// AUTO-GENERATED from the Handbook article markdown (scratchpad/handbook/out/*.md) via
// scratchpad/handbook/assemble.mjs. The built-in Handbook article bodies. Edit the source markdown
// and re-run the assembler, or edit here directly. Consumed by handbook-catalog.ts (seed) keyed by slug.

export const HANDBOOK_BODIES: Record<string, string> = {
  "api-route-conventions": "Every API route in Foundry looks the same on purpose. A reviewer should be able to open any file under `src/app/api/` and know, in ten seconds, how it authenticates, validates, and shapes its response — because it's the same four moves every time. This is the shape, and the reasoning behind it.\n\n## The canonical route\n\nA route handler does four things and nothing more: authenticate, validate, delegate, respond. It never contains business logic, and it never talks to Prisma directly.\n\n```ts\nimport { NextRequest } from \"next/server\";\nimport { apiOk, fromError } from \"@/lib/api-response\";\nimport { createClientRecord } from \"@/server/clients\";\nimport { clientCreateSchema } from \"@/server/validators\";\nimport { assertCan, canManageClients, getEffectiveUserOrNull } from \"@/server/auth/effective-user\";\n\nexport async function POST(request: NextRequest) {\n  try {\n    assertCan(await getEffectiveUserOrNull(request), canManageClients, \"create clients\");\n    const body = clientCreateSchema.parse(await request.json());\n    const client = await createClientRecord(body);\n    return apiOk({ client }, { status: 201 });\n  } catch (error) {\n    return fromError(error);\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThat's the whole pattern. Auth first, then Zod, then a single call into a `src/server/` module, then `apiOk`. Every error — validation, permission, or unexpected — funnels through one `catch` into `fromError`.\n\n## The four rules\n\nThese aren't style preferences. Break one and you've either opened a hole, leaked an inconsistent error shape, or buried logic where it can't be reused or tested.\n\n::: check EVERY ROUTE FOLLOWS\n- Respond via `apiOk` / `apiError` / `fromError` — never a raw `NextResponse.json`\n- Validate every body and query param with a Zod schema from `validators.ts`\n- Gate mutations with `assertCan` before touching data\n- Keep the handler thin — logic lives in `src/server/{domain}.ts`\n:::\n\n`fromError` is doing more than tidiness. It maps a `ZodError` to a 400 with the issues attached, honours the `.status` on `UnauthorizedError` (401) and `ForbiddenError` (403), and falls back to a 500 for anything else. Throw the right error deeper in the stack and the route reports the right code for free.\n\n## Next 15: params are a Promise\n\nIn the App Router, dynamic segments arrive as a `Promise`. Forgetting to `await` them is the single most common review catch on new routes.\n\n```ts\nexport async function GET(request: NextRequest, { params }: { params: Promise<{ slug: string }> }) {\n  try {\n    const { slug } = await params; // ← always await\n    const client = await getClientBySlug(slug);\n    if (!client) return apiError(\"Client not found\", 404);\n    return apiOk({ client });\n  } catch (error) {\n    return fromError(error);\n  }\n}\n```\n\nAlso note the static-vs-dynamic precedence trap: `/api/documents/analytics` is a static segment that wins over `/api/documents/[id]`. If you add a literal path that collides with a param route, the literal takes priority — name deliberately.\n\n## Auth is not optional\n\nEvery route under `/api/` is gated by the `API_KEY` env var in middleware — the one exception is `/api/health`. Browser traffic to `/app/**` carries a `gitwork_api_session` cookie; external and iOS callers send `Authorization: Bearer {API_KEY}`. That's the outer gate. It proves the request is *from us* — it says nothing about *who* is calling or what they may do.\n\nPer-user permission is the inner gate, and it's your job in the handler. Resolve the viewer, then `assertCan`. Note `assertCan(null, …)` is a deliberate no-op: a legacy shared-token or trusted server caller has no per-user identity and passes through, while a real user without the capability gets a `ForbiddenError`.\n\n> Gate the write, not just the button. A hidden UI control is a courtesy; the server check is the security boundary.\n\nNew capabilities default **off** — they're absent from the role matrix until switched on per member in Settings → Team. Never gate a sensitive read or any mutation on the frontend alone. See [Security baseline](/app/handbook) for the full trust model and [Handling secrets and credentials](/app/handbook) for where `API_KEY` lives.\n\n## Keep logic out of the route\n\nThe handler is glue. The moment you're mapping fields, running a second query, or making a decision, that belongs in the domain module. This keeps routes trivially reviewable, lets server logic be reused by crons and the iOS API, and keeps DTO shaping in one testable place. See [Data and Prisma conventions](/app/handbook) for how those modules scope queries and serialise rows.\n\n::: avoid NEVER IN A ROUTE HANDLER\n- `import { prisma }` and query the database directly\n- `return NextResponse.json(...)` with a bespoke error shape\n- Trust a request body without parsing it through Zod\n- Mutate data before checking permission\n- Inline field-mapping or business rules\n:::\n\n::: stats\n- Thin :: glue only, logic in the module\n- Validated :: Zod on every input\n- Gated :: auth before data, always\n- Consistent :: one response + error shape\n:::\n",
  "branching-and-commits": "On Foundry, `main` *is* production. Every push to `main` triggers a build, ships an image to GHCR, and deploys to the VPS in about six minutes. That one fact drives everything about how we branch and commit: `main` must always be deployable, and the history must read cleanly, because every commit becomes a line in the deploy feed everyone watches.\n\n## Two paths to main\n\nThere are exactly two ways work reaches `main`, and the choice is about blast radius.\n\n::: steps\n- Small fix → commit to a branch → PR → squash-merge\n- Feature → `feature/…` branch → PR → review → squash-merge\n:::\n\nEven a one-line fix goes through a branch and a PR — not because we don't trust you, but because a second pair of eyes is cheap and a bad deploy isn't. The only real difference between the two paths is size: a feature branch lives longer, gets reviewed harder, and may need [keeping in sync with `main`](/app/handbook) along the way.\n\nName branches for what they do: `feature/desk-mentions`, `fix/bulk-assign-scope`, `chore/bump-prisma`. Short, lowercase, hyphenated.\n\n## Squash-merge only\n\nMerge commits and rebase-merge are **disabled** on the repo. Every PR lands as exactly one commit on `main`, and merged branches auto-delete. This is deliberate.\n\n::: check WHY SQUASH-ONLY\n- One PR → one commit → one clean deploy line\n- History reads as a list of shipped changes, not a tangle\n- Reverting a change is reverting one commit\n- Your messy WIP commits never reach `main`\n- Branches clean themselves up on merge\n:::\n\nBecause the squash uses your **PR title** as the commit message, the title is load-bearing — it's what shows in the deploy feed and what you'll `git log` past in six months. Write it like it matters, because it does.\n\n> `main` is production. Never push anything to it you wouldn't be happy to see live in six minutes.\n\n## Conventional Commits\n\nEvery commit that lands on `main` (i.e. every PR title) is a [Conventional Commit](https://www.conventionalcommits.org): a type, an optional scope, and an imperative summary. This isn't ceremony — it's what makes the deploy feed scannable and tells everyone at a glance what shipped.\n\n```\nfeat(desk):  Needs You Today = reminders + @mentions + key mail only\nfix(wiki):   clamp Add New menu to the viewport\nfix(desk):   /desk resolves the workspace even when slackTeamId is unset\nchore(deps): bump next to 15.4\ndocs(handbook): add branching guide\n```\n\nRules of thumb: summary in the imperative (\"add\", not \"added\"), lowercase, no full stop, under ~70 characters. If you can't summarise it in one line, the PR is doing too much — split it.\n\n## Pick the right prefix\n\n| Prefix | Use it for |\n|---|---|\n| `feat` | New user-facing capability |\n| `fix` | A bug fix |\n| `chore` | Deps, tooling, config, housekeeping |\n| `docs` | Documentation only |\n| `refactor` | Restructuring with no behaviour change |\n| `perf` | A performance improvement |\n| `test` | Adding or fixing tests only |\n\nWhen in doubt between `feat` and `fix`, ask: was this behaviour ever meant to work? If yes, it's a `fix`; if it's genuinely new, it's a `feat`.\n\n## Keep it deployable\n\nBecause the merge is the deploy, a few habits are non-negotiable. Schema changes must be **additive only** — `prisma db push` runs without `--accept-data-loss`, and its safety check is all-or-nothing, so a single data-losing diff skips the *entire* sync. Never leave debug code, secrets, or half-built features on a commit bound for `main`.\n\n::: avoid NEVER ON MAIN\n- A commit that doesn't build\n- A data-losing schema change\n- Secrets, tokens or keys\n- Leftover debug logging\n- A half-finished feature behind no flag\n- A vague `updates` / `wip` message\n:::\n\nKeep the AI co-author trailer on your commit messages, sync feature branches by [rebasing not merging](/app/handbook), and prune stale branches as you go — see [Git hygiene & repo health](/app/handbook). Every clean commit is one more readable line in the story of what we shipped.\n",
  "building-on-the-design-system": "Foundry looks like one thing built by people who cared, not a stack of features bolted together — and that is entirely down to discipline with a small, opinionated design system. It has a signature you can spot across every module: numbered mono headers, an editorial serif for the big moments, a single blue, tight geometry, and a cream canvas instead of the usual clinical white. Learn the rules here and your screen will belong. Break them and it will stick out. `DESIGN.md` is the full law; this is the working guide.\n\n## The widget signature\n\nEvery widget and panel in the app wears the same badge: a numbered, monospaced, uppercase header — `01 // OVERVIEW`, `06 // DEVS`, `12 // MEETING NOTES`. It's the most recognisable thing about Foundry, and it's non-negotiable when you add a panel.\n\nThe numbering is sequential *within a view* and computed centrally, not hand-typed per widget — so the dashboards never show gaps or duplicates. When you add a widget, it slots into the sequence; you don't pick its number.\n\n```tsx\n// A widget header — mono, uppercase, numbered\n<h2 className=\"widget-header\">\n  <span className=\"widget-number\">14 //</span> ACTION ITEMS\n</h2>\n```\n\n::: check A WIDGET HEADER IS\n- Monospaced (JetBrains Mono)\n- Uppercase\n- Numbered `NN //` in sequence\n- Never plain sans-serif\n:::\n\nDashboards have their own rule that rides on top of this: fill every gap, keep the grid intact, and keep the `NN //` numbering sequential. A dashboard with a hole in the bento grid or a skipped number reads as broken even when it works.\n\n## The type triple\n\nThree typefaces, three jobs. Using the wrong one for a job is the fastest way to look off-brand.\n\n| Typeface | Used for | Never for |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Inter** | All UI text, body, buttons, forms | Editorial headlines |\n| **DM Serif Display** | Big stats, editorial titles, hero moments | Body copy or labels |\n| **JetBrains Mono** | Widget numbers, labels, timestamps, code | Paragraphs of prose |\n\nThe serif is a spice, not a staple — one editorial title, one big stat number. Reach for mono whenever something is a *label* or *data readout* (a date rail, an eyebrow, a metric caption), and it instantly reads as Foundry. A stat rendered in plain Inter looks like every other SaaS; the same stat in DM Serif Display with a mono caption looks like us.\n\n## Colour and shape\n\nOne brand colour: **Gitwork Blue** `#1D4ED8`. No second accent, no teal for success and orange for warnings sprinkled in for variety. Meaning comes from the blue family plus neutral, never from a rainbow.\n\n::: grid\n### Colour\n- Gitwork Blue `#1D4ED8` is the only accent\n- Stay in the blue family + neutrals\n- Cream canvas, never pure white\n- 6px status dots carry state, not colour sprawl\n### Shape\n- 6px radius on controls\n- 10px radius on cards\n- No pills — the only rounded-full thing is a 6px status dot\n- Tight, deliberate geometry\n:::\n\n::: avoid NEVER\n- A second accent colour outside the blue family\n- Pure white backgrounds\n- Pill-shaped buttons or tags\n- Random border-radii picked per component\n:::\n\nThe \"no pills\" rule surprises people. We don't do fully-rounded buttons or lozenge tags — the one exception is the 6px status dot. Everything else uses the 6px/10px geometry. It's a small constraint that does a lot of the \"these were designed together\" work.\n\n## Tokens, or dark mode breaks\n\nNever hard-code a colour. Style through CSS tokens — `--text-*`, `--surface-*`, `--border-*`, `--brand-*` — so the app themes correctly in both modes. This isn't optional polish; get it wrong and text literally disappears.\n\n```css\n/* Right — themed, survives dark mode */\n.panel {\n  color: var(--text-primary);\n  background: var(--surface-canvas);\n  border: 1px solid var(--border-subtle);\n}\n/* Wrong — invisible on the dark navy shell */\n.panel { color: #111; background: #fff; }\n```\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> The dark-mode remap covers `bg-white`, semantic tokens and `rgba(0,0,0,…)` borders — but **not** raw `neutral-*`, `slate-*`, `zinc-*` or `gray-*` Tailwind classes. Use a `gray-900` text colour and it stays near-black on the dark navy shell: invisible. Always reach for `--text-*` / `--surface-*` / `--border-*` tokens, never a raw neutral scale.\n\n## Tailwind v4 specifics\n\nWe're on Tailwind v4 — CSS-first config, no `tailwind.config.js`. Two things bite newcomers:\n\n- The anchor reset **must** live inside `@layer base { a { … } }`. Unlayered CSS overrides `text-{color}` utilities because of cascade-layer priority — this caused real invisible-link bugs on the marketing homepage.\n- Use `cn()` from `src/lib/format.ts` for conditional classes, and `app-select` / `app-select-compact` / `app-select-chevron` for every dropdown — the canonical chevron lives in `globals.css`. Never hand-roll select styling.\n\n## Editorial liberties\n\nThe system has room for personality — the Desk drawer uses serif-italic section rails and a hand-lettered stamp CTA — but liberties stay *on brand*: still blue, still cream, still the type triple. Personality is earned inside the rules, never by breaking the palette.\n\n> Constraints are the brand. The blue, the cream, the mono header and the tight geometry are what make Foundry feel like one product — protect them and improvise within them.\n\nCross-check [frontend standards](/app/handbook) for how these tokens fit component work, and [responsive and mobile standards](/app/handbook) for keeping the geometry intact across widths.\n\n::: stats\n- One blue :: `#1D4ED8`, no accent outside the family\n- Cream not white :: the canvas is warm, deliberate\n- Type triple :: Inter, DM Serif Display, JetBrains Mono\n- 6px / 10px :: controls and cards, no pills\n- Tokens always :: or dark mode goes invisible\n- `NN //` headers :: the signature on every widget\n\n:::\n",
  "client-handover-checklist": "A handover is the moment the client stops depending on you and starts owning the thing you built. Done well, they feel capable and in control; done badly, they're locked out of their own product and calling you every week for a password. The test of a good build isn't whether it works while you're holding it — it's whether it keeps working after you let go. This checklist is how we let go cleanly.\n\n## Build to be handed over\n\nThe best handover starts on day one, not at the end. If you build assuming someone else will run this — no secrets hard-coded, no undocumented manual steps, no \"only I know how to deploy it\" — the handover is mostly confirmation rather than a scramble.\n\n> A project you can't hand over isn't finished — it's just parked on you.\n\n::: check DESIGNED FOR HANDOVER FROM THE START\n- Secrets in the environment, never in code\n- Config documented as you go, not from memory\n- Deploys automated, not manual and tribal\n- Data model and decisions written down\n- Nothing that only works \"on your machine\"\n:::\n\n## Access — they own it, not you\n\nThe single most common handover failure is the client not actually owning their own accounts. Ownership sits with *them*; you have access to do the work, and that access can be revoked without breaking anything. Transfer or confirm ownership of every account before you call it done.\n\n::: grid\n### Accounts & ownership\n- Domain & DNS in the client's name\n- Hosting / infra owned by the client\n- Repository access transferred\n- Third-party services in their account\n### Credentials & secrets\n- Secrets live in the environment, not code\n- No personal accounts left as sole owner\n- Old/temporary access revoked\n- Rotation plan for anything you saw\n:::\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Never hand over by pasting credentials into Slack or email. Secrets belong in the environment (for Foundry, the VPS `.env`), shared through a proper secret channel. And remember: some values — like `ENCRYPTION_KEY` — must stay stable, because rotating them makes existing encrypted data unreadable.\n\n## Documentation — the answers without you\n\nGood docs answer the questions the client *will* ask, before they ask them. Write for the person who inherits this after the person you're handing to leaves. Store it where it lives with the work — the [Portal](/app/handbook) and the client's wiki — not in a document only you can find.\n\n::: check DOCS THAT MAKE THEM SELF-SUFFICIENT\n- How to run and deploy it\n- Architecture & key decisions, in plain terms\n- Environment variables and what each does\n- How to add a user / change a setting\n- Known limitations and what's out of scope\n- Who to contact for what, and how\n:::\n\n## Operations — it keeps running\n\nA live product has a heartbeat: backups, certificate renewals, scheduled jobs, monitoring. On our own infra these are self-managed on the box — the client (or whoever inherits ops) needs to know they exist and who's watching them. An expired TLS cert or a silently-failing cron is exactly the kind of thing that surfaces three weeks after you've moved on.\n\n::: check OPERATIONAL HANDOVER\n- Backups scheduled and *verified restorable*\n- Certificate renewal confirmed automatic\n- Scheduled jobs / crons documented\n- Monitoring & alerting in place\n- A written path for when something breaks\n:::\n\n::: avoid A HANDOVER ISN'T DONE IF\n- You're still the only one who can deploy\n- Accounts are in your name, not theirs\n- Secrets were shared over chat\n- The docs live only in your head\n- Nobody's confirmed a backup actually restores\n:::\n\n## The walkthrough — hand it over face to face\n\nFinish with a live session, not a link and a wave. Walk the client through running it, the docs, and the \"if X breaks, do Y\" path. Watch them do the key tasks themselves — that's how you find the gap your docs missed. Then agree what support looks like from here, and where the boundary sits. Keep the record of the walkthrough and any open items on the client's board so nothing falls through, and lean on [standups and communication](/app/handbook) to close out the last threads.\n\n::: stats\n- Owned by them :: every account, in their name\n- Documented :: run, deploy, decisions, contacts\n- Operable :: backups, certs, crons, monitoring\n- Secrets safe :: in the environment, never chat\n- Walked through :: they've done it themselves\n\n:::\n",
  "code-review-standards": "Code review is how we keep quality high and knowledge shared — not a gate, not a hazing ritual. Every change gets a second pair of eyes before it merges, because on Foundry a merge is a deploy. A good review is fast, kind, and specific. Both people have a job to do, and both jobs matter.\n\n## What review is for\n\nReview protects three things: correctness (does it work?), the codebase (will we still understand this in six months?), and each other (did we catch the mistake before a customer did?). It is *not* for showing off, relitigating settled conventions, or blocking on personal taste dressed up as principle.\n\n::: grid\n### As the reviewer\n- Understand the change before judging it\n- Run it in your head; check the edge cases\n- Confirm it's scoped to workspace & permission-gated\n- Praise what's good, not just what's wrong\n- Approve when it's right — don't gold-plate\n### As the author\n- Make the PR easy to review (see [Writing great PRs](/app/handbook))\n- Reply to every comment\n- Push follow-ups; don't force-push mid-review\n- Take the note or explain — kindly — why not\n- Own the change to production\n:::\n\n## Review the code you got\n\nReview the change in front of you, not the change you'd have written. If it's correct, clear, and consistent with the house style, approve it — even if you'd have named things differently. Your preferences are not the standard; [our conventions](/app/handbook) are. Save your energy for what actually matters.\n\n> Review the code you got, not the code you'd have written. Approve correct work you'd have done differently.\n\nThat said, some things you *must* block on. The line between a blocking issue and a passing comment is the difference between \"this will hurt someone\" and \"I'd have done it this way\".\n\n## Block or don't block\n\n| Block the merge | Leave a note, approve anyway |\n|---|---|\n| Security hole — unscoped query, missing `assertCan`, trusting client input | Naming you'd have chosen differently |\n| A real bug — wrong logic, unhandled error, N+1 | A cleaner-but-equivalent approach |\n| A data-losing schema diff (the [Prisma footgun](/app/handbook)) | A nice-to-have refactor for later |\n| Secrets, tokens or keys in the diff | Missing comment on obvious code |\n| No testing done, or obviously untested | Style the linter doesn't enforce |\n| Debug logging / test data left in | A follow-up idea out of scope |\n\nIf it's in the left column, say so plainly and don't approve until it's fixed. If it's in the right column, prefix it \"nit:\" or \"optional:\" so the author knows it isn't a blocker.\n\n## How to leave a comment\n\nBe specific and be kind — tone doesn't survive text, so err warm. Point at the exact line, explain the *why*, and where you can, suggest the fix. \"This query isn't scoped to the workspace — another client's rows will leak in; add `where: { workspaceId }`\" beats \"wrong\" every time.\n\n::: check A GOOD REVIEW COMMENT\n- Points at a specific line\n- Explains the consequence, not just the rule\n- Suggests a concrete fix where possible\n- Marks nits as nits\n- Asks a question when you're unsure\n:::\n\nAsk questions rather than issuing verdicts when you don't have the full picture — \"what happens if this is null?\" often teaches you something the author knows and you don't. Reviews are two-way learning.\n\n## Keep it moving\n\nA review is a favour with a deadline. Aim to look within a few hours, not days — a stalled PR drifts from `main`, gathers conflicts, and blocks the author. If you can't do a proper review now, say when you can, or hand it to someone who can. And approve when it's ready: sitting on a correct PR because you *might* find something is its own kind of waste.\n\n::: pills THE STANDARD\n- Reviewed within hours\n- Blocked only on real risk\n- Every comment answered\n- Kind, specific, actionable\n- Approved when it's right\n:::\n\nThe author merges once approved — see [Branching & commits](/app/handbook) for what happens next.\n",
  "code-style-and-conventions": "Consistency is a feature. When every file in Foundry follows the same conventions, anyone can open any part of the codebase and read it at speed — and code that reads fast is code that gets reviewed well and broken rarely. These are the house rules: how we name things, where things live, and which helpers you reach for instead of rolling your own.\n\nNone of this is about taste for its own sake. Each rule exists because the alternative caused a real problem once. Follow them and your PRs will feel native to the codebase from day one.\n\n## Naming\n\nNames are the first thing a reviewer reads. Get them right and half your code documents itself.\n\n| Thing | Convention | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| Component files | kebab-case `.tsx` | `client-detail.tsx` |\n| Components | PascalCase | `ClientDetail` |\n| Server modules | one per domain | `src/server/clients.ts` |\n| Hooks | `use-*` file, `useX` export | `use-tasks.ts` → `useTasks` |\n| Zod schemas | `xInput` / `xSchema` | `clientContactFields` |\n| Booleans | `is` / `has` / `can` prefix | `isShared`, `canViewCosts` |\n| Types / DTOs | PascalCase, separate from Prisma | `ClientMonthlyCost` |\n\nSay what a thing is, not how it's built. `pricedDevs` beats `arr2`. A name that needs a comment is usually the wrong name.\n\n## Structure\n\nFoundry has a place for everything, and code goes in its place. The layering is strict on purpose: it keeps business logic testable and routes thin.\n\n::: grid\n### Server logic\nOne module per domain at `src/server/{domain}.ts`. Agents live under `src/server/{domain}-agents/`, never at the root. DTOs are shaped separately from raw Prisma rows.\n### Routes stay thin\nAPI routes validate, authorise, and delegate. The real work happens in the server module they call — not inline in the handler.\n### Client data\nFetching goes through TanStack Query hooks in `src/hooks/`. Components consume hooks; they don't call `fetch` directly.\n### Validation at the edge\nEvery request body is parsed with a Zod schema from `src/server/validators.ts` before anything trusts it.\n:::\n\n## Reach for the house helpers\n\nHalf of writing idiomatic Foundry code is knowing what already exists. Don't hand-roll what the codebase gives you.\n\n::: check USE THESE, DON'T REINVENT\n- `apiOk` / `apiError` / `fromError` for every API response\n- `assertCan(user, canX, \"…\")` for permission gates\n- `cn()` from `src/lib/format.ts` for conditional classNames\n- Zod schemas from `src/server/validators.ts` for input\n- The `<Modal>` primitive and `useToast` for UI\n- CSS tokens (`--text-*`, `--surface-*`, `--border-*`) for colour\n:::\n\n::: avoid DON'T DO THIS\n- `NextResponse.json({...})` by hand instead of `apiOk`\n- Hardcoding an AI model name instead of resolving per-workspace\n- Raw hex colours instead of design-system tokens\n- A fresh toast library when `useToast` exists\n- Logic crammed into the route handler\n- `any` sprinkled to make the compiler quiet\n:::\n\n## A canonical API route\n\nThis is the shape. Validate, authorise, delegate, respond — in that order. Note `await params`: in Next 15, route params arrive as a Promise.\n\n```ts\nexport async function POST(\n  req: Request,\n  { params }: { params: Promise<{ slug: string }> },\n) {\n  try {\n    const { slug } = await params;              // Next 15: params is a Promise\n    const user = await requireAuthedUser(req);\n    assertCan(user, canManageClients, \"manage clients\");\n\n    const input = clientContactFields.parse(await req.json()); // Zod at the edge\n    const client = await updateClientRecord(slug, input);       // logic in the module\n\n    return apiOk(client);\n  } catch (error) {\n    return fromError(error);                    // maps status codes for you\n  }\n}\n```\n\n> The linter is not your opponent. It's the cheapest code reviewer you'll ever have — if it complains, it's usually right, so fix the code rather than silencing the rule.\n\n## TypeScript discipline\n\nTypes are documentation the compiler enforces. Keep them honest.\n\n- **`any` is the enemy.** It switches off the one tool that catches your mistakes for free. If you truly can't type something, use `unknown` and narrow it.\n- **Type the boundaries, infer the middle.** Annotate function inputs and returns; let TypeScript infer local variables.\n- **Prefer unions over optional soup.** A discriminated union beats five nullable fields that are only valid in certain combinations.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> Run `npx tsc --noEmit` before you push. Since the app is [hard to verify in a local browser](/app/handbook), a clean typecheck is your primary proof the change holds together.\n\nKeep changes small, keep them consistent, and hold them to the [Definition of Done](/app/handbook) before you open a PR. Consistency compounds — every file that follows the rules makes the next one easier to write.\n",
  "data-and-prisma-conventions": "Data is where a small mistake becomes an expensive one. A query that forgets a workspace filter leaks another client's records; a raw Prisma row returned from an API bakes your database shape into every consumer forever; an N+1 that's fine on your ten test rows falls over on production's ten thousand. These are the rules that keep Foundry's data layer safe and fast.\n\n## One client, imported from one place\n\nThere is exactly one Prisma client in the app, and you import it from `@/lib/prisma`. Never construct a `new PrismaClient()` anywhere else — in dev, Next's hot-reload would stack a fresh connection pool on every edit and exhaust Postgres in minutes.\n\n```ts\nimport { prisma } from \"@/lib/prisma\";\n// never: const prisma = new PrismaClient()\n```\n\nThe singleton also carries a dev-only slow-query logger that warns on anything over 300ms. If you see `[prisma slow-query]` in your terminal, that's a query asking to be indexed or narrowed — don't ignore it. See [SQL](/app/handbook) for indexing and the N+1 fixes.\n\n## Everything scopes to a workspace\n\nFoundry is multi-tenant. Almost every model hangs off a `workspaceId`, and almost every query must filter on it. A query without a tenant scope is a data leak waiting for its first real customer.\n\n::: check EVERY QUERY CONFIRMS\n- Scoped to the caller's `workspaceId`\n- Restricted developers further scoped to assigned clients\n- Sensitive fields gated by permission, not just hidden\n- Ownership re-checked on update and delete\n:::\n\nScoping is defence in depth, not a substitute for [permission checks](/app/handbook). A restricted developer is scoped to their `ClientAssignment` set *and* denied the mutation gate. Both, always.\n\n> A query without a workspace filter is a bug the size of your customer list.\n\n## Return DTOs, never raw rows\n\nA Prisma row is an internal implementation detail. The moment it crosses an API boundary it becomes a contract — with the web app, with iOS, with every future integration. Map it through a `serialize*` function in the domain module so you choose exactly what leaves the server and can change the schema without breaking clients.\n\n```ts\nexport function serializeRateCardPerson(person: RateCardPerson): RateCardPersonRecord {\n  return {\n    id: person.id,\n    name: person.name,\n    role: person.role,\n    // rate is deliberately omitted here — gated behind code.viewRates\n    createdAt: person.createdAt.toISOString(),\n  };\n}\n```\n\nThis is also where sensitive fields get dropped for viewers who can't see them, and where `Date` becomes an ISO string. Never `return apiOk({ person })` with a bare Prisma object.\n\n## Keep schema changes additive\n\nFoundry's deploy runs `prisma db push` **without** `--accept-data-loss`. The safety check is all-or-nothing per push: if the diff contains *anything* Prisma judges data-losing — even an unrelated dropped column on a sibling table — the **entire** sync is skipped, additive parts included. Your Prisma client generates fine, TypeScript compiles, and the live column silently never appears, so `upsert` crashes at runtime with \"column does not exist\".\n\n::: check SAFE TO SHIP\n- New table\n- New nullable column\n- New column with a default\n- New index or relation\n:::\n\n::: avoid WON'T APPLY ON DEPLOY\n- Dropping a column or table\n- Renaming a field (drop + add)\n- Removing an enum value\n- Tightening nullable → required\n:::\n\nBefore merging a schema change, run `git diff origin/main -- prisma/schema.prisma` and confirm it's purely additive. If a drop is genuinely needed, apply it manually against prod first or switch to a proper migration — see [Database changes and the Prisma footgun](/app/handbook) for the full drill.\n\n## Performance traps\n\nMost data bugs aren't logic errors — they're queries that don't scale. Learn to spot these in review.\n\n| Trap | Symptom | Fix |\n|---|---|---|\n| N+1 | A query inside a `.map`/loop | `include`/`select` the relation, or one `findMany` + group in memory |\n| Over-fetching | `findMany` with no `select` | Select only the fields the DTO uses |\n| Counting in JS | `findMany().length` | `prisma.x.count()` or `groupBy` |\n| Unindexed filter | Slow-query warnings | Add an index on the column you filter/sort |\n| Batched writes in a loop | Sequential `await` per row | `createMany` / `updateMany` / `$transaction` |\n\nDo the counting and aggregation in Postgres — it's built for it, and moving that work into Node just ships every row over the wire to add them up.\n\n::: stats\n- Singleton :: one Prisma client, imported\n- Scoped :: workspace filter on every query\n- Shaped :: DTOs out, never raw rows\n- Additive :: schema changes that always apply\n:::\n",
  "database-changes-prisma-footgun": "There is one way to break production with a change that compiles cleanly, passes review, deploys green, and still leaves your feature crashing at runtime: a non-additive Prisma schema diff. The deploy runs `prisma db push` **without** `--accept-data-loss`, and that safety check is all-or-nothing. Understand this before you edit `schema.prisma` — it has bitten us for real.\n\n## Why one bad diff skips everything\n\n`prisma db push` inspects the whole diff between your schema and the live database. If *any* part of it would lose data — a dropped column, a renamed field, a tightened type, even on a table you never touched — Prisma refuses the entire sync. Not just the risky part. **All of it, additive changes included.**\n\nSo a \"purely additive\" PR (new table, new nullable column) can still fail to reach prod if the schema *also* carries an unrelated pending drop from an earlier commit. The Prisma client generates fine, TypeScript compiles, the build goes green — but the live DB never gets your column.\n\n::: steps\n- You add a nullable column\n- Schema also carries an old drop\n- db push sees a data-losing diff\n- The WHOLE sync is skipped\n- Your column never lands\n- upsert() crashes: \"column does not exist\"\n:::\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> This is not theoretical. In PR #189 a benign additive change never applied because the schema carried an unrelated drop. Live `upsert()` calls failed with *\"the column does not exist in the current database\"* — a green deploy, a broken feature.\n\n## Additive is safe. Everything else is a decision.\n\nThe rule of thumb: if your diff only *adds*, it applies cleanly on deploy. The moment it *removes or changes* anything, it won't — and you must handle it deliberately.\n\n| Change | Applies via deploy? |\n|---|---|\n| New table | ✅ Yes |\n| New nullable column | ✅ Yes |\n| New column with a default | ✅ Yes |\n| New index / relation | ✅ Yes |\n| Drop a column | ❌ No — skips the whole sync |\n| Rename a field | ❌ No — read as drop + add |\n| Tighten a type / make required | ❌ No — potential data loss |\n| Remove an enum value | ❌ No |\n\n::: check DEFAULT TO ADDITIVE\n- Add nullable columns, don't repurpose old ones\n- Keep legacy fields around, mark them dead in code\n- New enum values, never remove old ones\n- Backfill in a separate follow-up\n:::\n\n::: avoid DON'T\n- Drop or rename in a \"quick\" schema tweak\n- Assume a green build means the DB synced\n- Bundle a cleanup drop with a feature migration\n- Trust that TypeScript compiling means prod is fine\n:::\n\n## Before you merge a schema change\n\nTwo minutes of checking saves a production incident. Diff your schema against `main` and look for anything that isn't a pure add.\n\n```bash\ngit diff origin/main -- prisma/schema.prisma\n# Scan for removed columns, tables, enum values, indices, FKs.\n# All additive AND main matches prod? It'll apply cleanly.\n```\n\nIf the diff contains **any** drop or rename — yours or one pending from an earlier commit — pick one:\n\n1. **Apply manually first.** Pull the prod `DATABASE_URL`/`DIRECT_URL` and run `npx prisma db push --accept-data-loss` from your machine. Verify the DB, *then* merge. In the #189 incident this fix took about ten seconds.\n2. **Switch to real migrations.** Use `prisma migrate` to generate and commit the SQL; the deploy runs `migrate deploy`. Heavier, but reproducible — the right move once schema changes get frequent.\n\n> When in doubt, add. A dropped column is a production incident waiting for a runtime call. A dead-but-present column is just tidy-up you can do later.\n\nSee [deploying Foundry](/app/handbook) for how the `db push` step fits the pipeline, and [incident response](/app/handbook) for the emergency manual-apply.\n\n::: stats\n- Additive :: applies on every deploy, safely\n- All-or-nothing :: one drop skips the whole sync\n- Green ≠ applied :: build passing doesn't mean DB synced\n- Check the diff :: before every merge, no exceptions\n\n:::\n",
  "definition-of-done": "\"Done\" is the most abused word in software. It usually means \"the code compiles on my machine and I'm tired of looking at it\". At Gitwork it means something specific and shared: the change works, it's been tested, it's clean, and it's safe to put in front of a paying customer. When you say \"done\" in the channel, this is the promise you're making.\n\nBecause every push to `main` deploys straight to production, there is no safety net between \"done\" and \"live\". The word has to carry real weight. This article is what it weighs.\n\n## What Done means\n\nA change is done when all of this is true — not most of it, all of it. If you can't tick every item, it isn't done; it's in progress.\n\n::: check A CHANGE IS DONE WHEN\n- It does what the task asked, edge cases included\n- You've tested it yourself — happy path and failure path\n- `npm run build` and `npm run lint` pass clean\n- Types check (`tsc --noEmit`), no `any` bandaids\n- Forms validate; success and error states both handled\n- Loading, empty and populated states all render\n- Responsive at real widths, no console errors\n- Permissions checked; no data leaks to the wrong role\n- Debug logging, test data and dead code removed\n- A reviewer has approved it\n:::\n\n::: avoid NOT DONE, WHATEVER YOU CALL IT\n- \"It works on my machine\"\n- \"It's basically finished, just needs testing\"\n- Debug `console.log`s left in\n- The happy path only — errors untested\n- One screen size checked\n- A red build you \"just need to fix\"\n- Placeholder copy or test data still in\n- No review yet\n:::\n\n## Levels of done\n\nNot every \"done\" is the same done. Be precise about which one you mean — the ambiguity is where trust leaks.\n\n| You say | People hear | It actually means |\n|---|---|---|\n| \"Code's written\" | Nothing to rely on | Compiles, untested |\n| \"It's working\" | You can demo it | Happy path runs locally |\n| \"Self-tested\" | Ready for review | You've hit the edges yourself |\n| \"Reviewed\" | Almost there | Approved, not yet merged |\n| **\"Done\"** | **It's live and safe** | Merged, deployed, verified in prod |\n\nOnly the last row earns the word \"done\". Everything above it is a status update, so give it its real name.\n\n> Done is a word we protect. If everyone uses it loosely, it stops meaning anything — and then nobody can trust a status update, and we're back to checking each other's work by hand.\n\n## Verify it, don't assume it\n\nThe step people skip is the last one: confirming the change actually works where it counts. Merging is not the finish line — a merge you never verified is a guess with your name on it.\n\n::: steps\n- Self-test\n- Get reviewed\n- Squash-merge\n- Watch deploy\n- Verify live\n:::\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> Because `main` deploys to real customers in about six minutes, \"verify live\" is part of done, not a nice-to-have. Merge, watch the deploy land, then check the actual behaviour in production. If you can't safely verify something in prod, raise it before you merge — don't ship on hope.\n\n## Before you call it done\n\nA final gut-check. Say these out loud in the channel — the same release sign-off habit we use everywhere. If any of them is shaky, the honest move is to [say so early](/app/handbook) rather than stretch the word.\n\n::: pills BEFORE YOU CALL IT DONE\n- Build passes\n- Self-tested\n- Reviewed & approved\n- Deployed\n- Verified in production\n- No known critical issues\n:::\n\nDone isn't a feeling of completion; it's a checklist that protects the customer and the person who reviews your next PR. Hold the line on it here and it holds everywhere else. For the mechanics of getting there cleanly, see [code style and conventions](/app/handbook) and the [first-week checklist](/app/handbook).\n\n::: stats\n- Works :: edge cases included\n- Tested :: by you, both paths\n- Clean :: no debug, no dead code\n- Reviewed :: approved by a human\n- Live :: deployed and verified\n:::\n",
  "deploying-foundry": "Foundry has no deploy button. There is `git push`, and there is production. Push to `main` and roughly six minutes later your code is live for real clients on the VPS — no staging gate, no manual promote, no one to catch it for you. That directness is a gift and a loaded gun, so learn exactly what happens when you push.\n\n## What actually happens on push\n\nEvery push to `main` triggers a GitHub Actions workflow (`.github/workflows/deploy.yml`). It builds a Docker image, ships it to the registry, then SSHes into the Fasthosts VPS and swaps the running container. There is no human in the loop once you push.\n\n::: steps\n- git push to main\n- GitHub Actions builds image\n- Push image to GHCR\n- SSH to the VPS\n- docker compose pull\n- prisma db push\n- Restart the app (~6 min)\n:::\n\nThe `prisma db push` step runs **without** `--accept-data-loss`. That single fact governs how you write schema changes — read [database changes and the Prisma footgun](/app/handbook) before you touch `schema.prisma`, because a bad diff silently skips the *entire* sync and your new column never lands.\n\n## What lives where\n\nProduction is a single VPS running Docker Compose, not a managed platform. When something breaks, it helps to know which box owns which job.\n\n| Concern | Where it lives |\n|---|---|\n| App + Postgres | Docker Compose on the Fasthosts VPS |\n| DNS | `foundry.gitwork.co.uk` A record (Squarespace) |\n| TLS | Let's Encrypt on the reverse proxy (self-renewed) |\n| Secrets | The VPS `.env` — never git, never Slack |\n| Images | GitHub Container Registry (GHCR) |\n| Crons / DB backups | Self-managed on the box (no platform to do it for us) |\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> There is no separate staging environment and no `--accept-data-loss`. The safety net you might expect from a managed host is *you*, before you push.\n\n## Watch your deploy\n\nPushing and walking away is how a broken build sits live for an hour before anyone notices. Own the change through to green.\n\n::: check AFTER YOU PUSH\n- Watch the Actions run go green\n- Load the live page you changed\n- Check the one flow you touched actually works\n- Confirm no errors in the container logs\n- Tell the channel it's out\n:::\n\n::: avoid DON'T\n- Push at 5pm on a Friday and log off\n- Assume a green build means a working feature\n- Push a schema change you haven't checked for drops\n- Ship secrets or debug logging to prod\n:::\n\nYour commit message is the deploy-feed label — it's what everyone reads to understand what just went live. Write it in [Conventional Commits](/app/handbook) style (`fix:`, `feat:`, `chore:`), descriptive enough that someone scanning the feed knows what changed and why.\n\n## When it goes wrong\n\nMost bad deploys are fixed by shipping the correct code forward, fast. Because every push deploys, a one-line `fix:` commit is often live before a rollback would have finished.\n\n```bash\n# Roll forward — usually fastest\ngit commit -m \"fix: guard null client on desk drawer\"\ngit push            # ~6 min to live\n\n# True rollback: repoint or redeploy a known-good image (GHCR :sha tag)\n```\n\nIf the outage is a crashing route caused by a schema column that never applied, that's the Prisma footgun biting — apply the additive change manually against the prod `DATABASE_URL`, then reconcile. Full playbook in [incident response](/app/handbook).\n\n> Every push is a release. Treat the six minutes after it as part of your job, not someone else's.\n\n::: stats\n- One command :: git push is the whole pipeline\n- ~6 minutes :: build to live\n- No staging :: you are the gate\n- Additive schema :: or the sync silently skips\n- Watch it green :: own the change to production\n:::\n",
  "first-week-checklist": "Your first week has one goal: get one small change merged and deployed to production. Not a big feature — a real, useful, tiny change that walks the whole path from your machine to a customer's screen. Do that, and you've learned the pipeline the only way that sticks. This is the day-by-day route to get there without guessing.\n\nDon't rush it and don't stall on it. If something blocks you, [say the true thing early](/app/handbook) — ask in the channel the same day. Nobody expects you to be self-sufficient in week one; they expect you to be honest about where you're stuck.\n\n## The week at a glance\n\nEach day builds on the last. By Friday you'll have shipped.\n\n::: steps\n- Get set up\n- Read the standards\n- Pick a small task\n- Open a PR\n- Merge & deploy\n:::\n\n## Day 1 — get set up\n\nGet Foundry running locally and get your accounts in order before you try to write anything. A broken environment on day one is normal; fighting it silently is not.\n\n::: check DAY ONE\n- Foundry runs locally on :3000 (see [local setup](/app/handbook))\n- `.env.local` has real values and the app boots\n- GitHub access to `Git-Dann/docs-by-gitwork` confirmed\n- Slack, Google account and the task board all reachable\n- You've said hello in the team channel\n:::\n\n> [!TIP]\n> The [local environment setup](/app/handbook) article has the exact commands and env-var table. Work through it top to bottom rather than improvising — the two-URL Postgres setup and the encryption key trip people up.\n\n## Day 2 — read the standards\n\nRead, don't skim. These are the articles you'll be measured against in review, so learning them now saves you re-work later.\n\n- [Welcome — how we work](/app/handbook) — the five principles\n- [Code style and conventions](/app/handbook) — naming, structure, house helpers\n- [Definition of Done](/app/handbook) — what \"finished\" actually means\n\nWhile you read, poke around the codebase with the app running. Open `src/server/`, `src/hooks/` and `src/components/`. Follow one feature end to end — say, a Portal client card — from the React component to the hook to the API route to the server module. Understanding one vertical slice teaches you the shape of all of them.\n\n## Day 3 — pick a small task\n\nGrab something genuinely small from the board or ask your lead to point you at a starter task. A copy fix, a missing loading state, a tidy-up. The point is the pipeline, not the difficulty.\n\n::: avoid DON'T MAKE THIS HARD\n- A sprawling feature \"to prove yourself\"\n- A change touching ten files across three modules\n- Something you can't test yourself\n- A refactor with no clear finish line\n:::\n\nCut a branch (`fix/…` or `feat/…`), make the change, and self-test it properly against the [Definition of Done](/app/handbook). If you haven't tested it, it isn't ready to show anyone.\n\n## Day 4 — open a pull request\n\nA PR is writing aimed at a reviewer. Say what changed, why, and how you tested it. Keep it small enough that someone can review it over a coffee.\n\n::: check BEFORE YOU REQUEST REVIEW\n- `npm run build` passes locally\n- `npm run lint` is clean\n- No debug logging or test data left behind\n- A clear, [conventional-commit](/app/handbook) title (`fix:` / `feat:`)\n- You've described what you tested\n:::\n\nThen respond to feedback quickly and without ego. Review is how we [leave the codebase better](/app/handbook); it's not a personal audit.\n\n## Day 5 — merge and deploy\n\nOnce approved, **squash-merge** it. That single commit lands on `main`, and `main` deploys to the Fasthosts VPS automatically in about six minutes. Watch the deploy through — don't merge and walk away.\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> Every push to `main` goes to real customers. There is no separate \"deploy\" button and no safety staging gate — the merge *is* the release. Treat it with the respect that deserves.\n\nOnce it's live, check the change actually works in production. That last step — verifying live, not assuming — is the habit that separates people who ship from people who cause incidents.\n\n::: stats\n- Set up :: environment running clean\n- Learned :: the standards, not skimmed\n- Shipped :: one small change, merged\n- Deployed :: live and verified in prod\n- Confident :: you know the pipeline now\n:::\n\nWelcome aboard. After this week, everything else in the handbook is just refinement.\n",
  "frontend-standards": "Good Foundry frontend code is boring in the best way: it fetches data the one right way, reuses the primitives we already have, and never surprises the user with a blank screen or a spinner that runs forever. We're on Next.js 15 (App Router), React 19, TypeScript and Tailwind v4 — this is how we build on it so screens feel like one product, not twelve.\n\n## Data fetching is React Query, always\n\nServer state lives in TanStack React Query v5. Don't hand-roll `useEffect` + `fetch` + `useState` — you'll reinvent caching, loading, error and invalidation badly. Every domain already has hooks in `src/hooks/`; add to them rather than fetching inline.\n\n```ts\n// A hook in src/hooks/, wrapping a fetcher from src/lib/api.ts\nexport function useClientTasks(slug: string) {\n  return useQuery({\n    queryKey: [\"tasks\", slug],\n    queryFn: () => getClientTasks(slug),\n  });\n}\n\n// Mutations invalidate — never window.location.reload()\nconst { mutate } = useMutation({\n  mutationFn: updateTask,\n  onSuccess: () => queryClient.invalidateQueries({ queryKey: [\"tasks\", slug] }),\n});\n```\n\n::: check EVERY QUERY HANDLES\n- The loading state (skeleton, not layout shift)\n- The error state (a message, not a blank div)\n- The empty state (a prompt, not zero rows)\n- Invalidation on mutate (no manual reload)\n:::\n\n## The three states, every time\n\nThe single most common frontend bug in an internal tool is a component that only renders the happy path. A list with data looks great in dev; the same list renders as nothing, or a perpetual spinner, the moment the query is empty or fails.\n\n::: grid\n### Loading\n- Show a skeleton that matches the final layout\n- No content jump when data arrives\n- Never an infinite spinner with no timeout path\n### Empty\n- Say what's missing and how to add it\n- A call to action, not a void\n### Populated\n- The real thing, responsive, accessible\n- Errors surfaced inline, not swallowed\n:::\n\n> A screen that only renders when the data is perfect isn't finished — it's a demo.\n\n## Reuse the primitives\n\nWe have shared UI in `src/components/ui/` — `Modal` (focus trap, Escape, scroll-lock, ARIA), `toast`, buttons, selects. Use them. A hand-rolled dialog or a bespoke `<select>` is how the app drifts out of alignment with the [design system](/app/handbook).\n\n::: check REACH FOR THESE FIRST\n- `<Modal>` for every dialog\n- `useToast()` for every notification\n- `app-select` / `app-select-compact` for dropdowns\n- `cn()` from `src/lib/format.ts` for conditional classes\n:::\n\n::: avoid DON'T\n- Hand-roll a modal or a select\n- Add a toast library — we have one\n- Style a component when a token exists\n- Reach for a new dependency before checking `ui/`\n:::\n\n## Images and accessibility\n\nTwo rules that catch most of the avoidable review comments.\n\nUse `next/image` (`<Image>`) for everything static in `public/`. Raw `<img>` is only for dynamic, user-uploaded content (avatars, receipts) — and when you use it, disable the lint rule on the line above so the exception is deliberate:\n\n```tsx\n{/* eslint-disable-next-line @next/next/no-img-element */}\n<img src={user.avatarUrl} alt={user.name} />\n```\n\nAccessibility isn't a polish pass. Buttons are `<button>`, not clickable `<div>`s. Every input has a label. Every image has meaningful `alt`. Interactive elements are keyboard-reachable and focus-visible — the `Modal` primitive already gets this right, which is another reason to use it.\n\n::: check A11Y BASELINE\n- Real semantic elements (button, nav, label)\n- Every input labelled\n- Meaningful alt text\n- Keyboard + focus states work\n:::\n\nStyle with tokens (`--text-*`, `--surface-*`, `--border-*`), never raw hex, so dark mode works for free — the details and the dark-mode invisible-text trap are covered in [building on the design system](/app/handbook). And when a layout needs to work at every width, follow [responsive and mobile standards](/app/handbook) rather than patching one breakpoint.\n\n::: stats\n- One data path :: React Query, no bespoke fetching\n- Three states :: loading, empty, populated — always\n- Reuse first :: primitives over hand-rolled UI\n- Tokens not hex :: dark mode for free\n- Accessible :: semantic, labelled, keyboard-ready\n\n:::\n",
  "git-hygiene-and-repo-health": "Every Claude Code session spins up a fresh git worktree, and left unchecked those worktrees and stale local branches pile up until the object store corrupts — missing delta bases, orphaned commits, a `git gc` that refuses to run. This isn't hypothetical: it happened, and fixing it needed a full mirror clone. A few minutes of routine maintenance keeps the repo fast and healthy; this article is how.\n\n## Why it rots\n\nThe repo accumulates cruft from two sides. Worktrees pile up because sessions create them and rarely clean them up. Local `claude/` branches accumulate because they're created per-session and never deleted. Together they bloat `.git`, slow every command, and eventually leave the pack files in a state `git gc` and `git fsck` can't repair.\n\nThe good news: it's entirely preventable with a periodic sweep. The bad news: if you skip it long enough, the recovery is a proper job. Do the sweep.\n\n## Routine maintenance\n\nRun this every few sessions — or any time you notice more than about five active worktrees. **Run it from the main repo directory, never from inside a worktree:**\n\n```bash\n# Prune worktrees whose directories are gone\ngit worktree prune\n\n# Delete stale local claude/ branches\ngit branch | grep claude/ | xargs git branch -D 2>/dev/null || true\n\n# Garbage-collect and drop unreachable objects now\ngit gc --prune=now\n```\n\nThat's the whole ritual. It takes seconds on a healthy repo and keeps it that way.\n\n> Tidy the repo on a schedule, not in a crisis. The mirror-clone rescue is what happens when nobody swept.\n\n::: check KEEP IT TIDY\n- Prune worktrees every few sessions\n- Delete merged & stale local branches\n- Let auto-delete clear remote branches on merge\n- `git gc` before the pile gets big\n- Run maintenance from the main repo, not a worktree\n:::\n\nYou get help here for free: because we [squash-merge](/app/handbook) with auto-delete on, merged *remote* branches clean themselves up. It's the *local* branches and worktrees you have to sweep by hand.\n\n## When gc fails\n\nIf `git gc` errors with \"bad tree object\" or \"unable to read [sha]\", the object store is already corrupt and no amount of pruning will fix it. Do **not** reach for a shallow clone to repair it — shallow packs have their own missing bases and will make things worse. The reliable fix is a full mirror clone, copying its intact pack files over your broken ones:\n\n```bash\n# 1. Full mirror clone (NOT --depth — shallow packs are broken too)\ngit clone --mirror https://github.com/Git-Dann/docs-by-gitwork.git /tmp/repair\n\n# 2. Copy ALL pack files over the corrupt ones\ncp /tmp/repair/objects/pack/*.pack .git/objects/pack/\ncp /tmp/repair/objects/pack/*.idx  .git/objects/pack/\n\n# 3. Clean up\nrm -rf /tmp/repair\n\n# 4. Retry\ngit gc --prune=now\n```\n\nThe mirror has a complete, uncorrupted copy of every object; dropping its packs in gives your local repo the delta bases it was missing.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Never use `git clone --depth=20` to repair a corrupt store. Shallow-clone packs are themselves missing bases, so you'll trade one broken repo for another. Full mirror clone, every time.\n\n::: avoid WHAT MAKES IT WORSE\n- Letting worktrees pile up unpruned\n- Hoarding dozens of dead local branches\n- Running `git gc` from inside a worktree\n- Using `--depth` to repair corruption\n- Ignoring \"bad tree object\" and pushing anyway\n:::\n\n## Make it a habit\n\nNone of this is hard; it's just easy to forget until the repo grinds to a halt. Fold the routine sweep into your rhythm — end of a long session, start of a new feature, whenever things feel sluggish. A healthy `.git` means fast `status`, fast `rebase`, and no nasty surprises mid-deploy.\n\n::: stats\n- Pruned :: no orphaned worktrees\n- Lean :: stale branches deleted\n- Packed :: gc'd before it bloats\n- Recoverable :: mirror clone if it ever breaks\n:::\n\nPair this with [keeping your branch in sync](/app/handbook) and clean [branching & commits](/app/handbook), and the repo stays a pleasure to work in rather than a liability.\n",
  "handling-secrets-and-credentials": "A leaked secret is one of the few mistakes that can't be quietly rolled back — once a key is in a git history or a Slack thread, you have to assume it's compromised and rotate it. Foundry has a small number of hard rules about where credentials live and how they move. Learn them once; they never change.\n\n## Where secrets live\n\nThere is one source of truth for production secrets: the **VPS `.env`**, loaded by Docker Compose. Not Vercel, not a config file in the repo, not a pinned Slack message. Local development uses `.env.local`, which is gitignored and yours alone.\n\n| Secret | Purpose | Lives in |\n|---|---|---|\n| `DATABASE_URL` / `DIRECT_URL` | Postgres connection | VPS `.env` (compose-internal) |\n| `API_KEY` | Gates all `/api/` routes | VPS `.env` / `.env.local` |\n| `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` (+ OpenAI/Gemini) | AI providers | VPS `.env`, or per-workspace record |\n| `ENCRYPTION_KEY` | AES-256-GCM for bank details | VPS `.env` — **must stay stable** |\n| `GITHUB_TOKEN` | Pulse scans, fix-agent PRs | VPS `.env` |\n| `CRON_SECRET` | Guards `/api/cron/*` | VPS `.env` |\n| Per-client analytics / Slack tokens | Care connectors | DB, on the connection record — not env |\n\nNote the last row: not every credential is an env var. A client's product-analytics token lives server-side on its `AccountConnection`, and Slack tokens live on the workspace record. Secrets belong in exactly one place each — know which.\n\n## The hard rules\n\nThese are absolute. There's no \"just this once\" — a secret that touches the wrong channel is burned and must be rotated.\n\n::: avoid NEVER\n- Commit a secret to git — not even in a branch you'll \"clean up later\"\n- Paste a key or password into Slack, a PR, or an issue\n- Log a secret, token, or password\n- Send a raw secret to the client or a third party\n- Read a production value into your local shell history\n- Hardcode a key in source when it should be an env var\n:::\n\nIf a secret does end up somewhere it shouldn't, rotating it is the *only* fix. Deleting the message or force-pushing the commit doesn't help — assume it was read the moment it was posted. Rotate, then tell the channel. See [Security baseline](/app/handbook) for the full disclosure drill.\n\n> A secret in the wrong place isn't a secret anymore. Rotate it — don't hope nobody saw.\n\n## The ENCRYPTION_KEY is sacred\n\n`ENCRYPTION_KEY` is a 32-byte base64 secret (generate with `openssl rand -base64 32`) that encrypts client bank details at rest via `src/lib/encryption.ts`. It uses AES-256-GCM with a versioned JSON envelope, so the algorithm can be rotated without a destructive migration — but the *key itself* is a different matter.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> If `ENCRYPTION_KEY` changes, every value encrypted with the old key becomes permanently unrecoverable. There is no reset, no recovery, no support ticket. During the Fasthosts migration it was recovered from a local worktree and verified byte-for-byte against prod *before* cutover, precisely because a mismatch would have silently destroyed every stored bank detail. Treat it as immutable.\n\nThe code enforces its own shape — it throws if the key is missing or doesn't decode to exactly 32 bytes — so a misconfigured environment fails loudly at boot rather than corrupting data silently. Trust that error; don't work around it by generating a fresh key on prod.\n\n## Access hygiene\n\nSecrets should be inconvenient to reach and boring to handle. That's the point.\n\n::: check GOOD HYGIENE\n- Pull a value only when you actually need it, then let it go\n- Prefer env vars over reading prod interactively\n- Grant the minimum scope on any token you mint\n- Rotate on any suspected exposure, and after anyone leaves\n- Keep `.env.local` out of screen-shares and pair sessions\n:::\n\nWhen you need a new secret in production, add it to the VPS `.env` and restart the app container — don't smuggle it through a commit or a chat message to get it there faster. When you generate a token for GitHub or a client integration, scope it to exactly the permissions the feature needs and no more; a `repo`-scoped PAT that only needs read access is a liability, not a convenience.\n\nThe `docs/fasthosts-secrets-recovery.md` runbook documents where each production secret is sourced from — without ever listing a live value. That's the model for how we talk about secrets: precise about *where*, silent about *what*.\n\n::: stats\n- One home :: VPS `.env` for prod, `.env.local` for you\n- Never :: git, Slack, logs, or the client\n- Immutable :: ENCRYPTION_KEY stays stable, forever\n- Least scope :: tokens grant the minimum, rotate on doubt\n:::\n",
  "incident-response": "Production is a live environment with real customer data, and every push to `main` deploys to it in about six minutes. That speed is a gift most of the time and a liability when something breaks. When it does — a crash, a bad deploy, a route throwing 500s — the difference between a five-minute blip and an afternoon of chaos is almost entirely about how calmly you work the first ten minutes. This is that playbook.\n\n## First, stay calm and take one seat\n\nAn incident with three people all \"helping\" in different directions is worse than one person working it methodically. The first thing to establish is who's driving. That person coordinates; everyone else offers information when asked and otherwise stays out of the shell.\n\n::: steps\n- Assess\n- Stabilise\n- Fix\n- Learn\n:::\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> Stabilise before you fix. Getting customers back to a working state — even a degraded one — always beats a clever root-cause fix that takes an hour. A fast rollback now buys you the calm to fix it properly later.\n\n## Assess — what's actually broken\n\nBefore touching anything, get the shape of the problem. Guessing wastes the minutes that matter most.\n\n::: check ESTABLISH FAST\n- What's broken, for whom, since when\n- Is it *all* customers or one client/route\n- Did it start at a deploy? Check the deploy feed\n- Is data at risk, or just availability\n- The actual error — logs on the VPS, not a hunch\n:::\n\nBecause production runs on the [Fasthosts VPS via Docker Compose](/app/handbook), the truth is in the container logs and the deploy history, not in your local memory of what \"should\" be happening. If the timing lines up with a recent squash-merge, you've very likely found your cause.\n\n## Stabilise — stop the bleeding\n\nYour goal here is a working product for customers, not a correct one for you.\n\n| Situation | Fastest safe move |\n|---|---|\n| Bad deploy just landed | Revert the commit on `main` — it redeploys clean |\n| A route is crashing | Guard/short-circuit it, ship the guard |\n| Missing DB column at runtime | Apply the additive change manually (below) |\n| Whole app down, cause unclear | `docker compose` restart, then investigate |\n| Rollback path unclear | Repoint to the read-only Vercel/Neon fallback |\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> The classic production crash is a route reading a column the live DB never got, because a non-additive schema change silently skipped `prisma db push` (the all-or-nothing safety check). The ten-second fix: pull the prod `DATABASE_URL`/`DIRECT_URL` and run `npx prisma db push --accept-data-loss` from your machine to apply the additive part. See [the Prisma footgun](/app/handbook).\n\nReverting is not a defeat. A revert is a completely normal, healthy response to a bad deploy — it's the system working as designed.\n\n## Fix — properly, once it's calm\n\nWith customers stable, you have room to do it right. Reproduce the failure, understand *why* it happened (not just what), write the fix, and verify it the way you'd verify any change — happy path, edge cases, no console errors. Don't skip QA because it's an incident; incident fixes shipped in a panic are how you get a second incident.\n\n::: avoid DURING AN INCIDENT, NEVER\n- Fix live by poking production data by hand\n- Ship a \"quick fix\" you haven't verified\n- Debug silently while others wait for word\n- Run a destructive DB change under pressure\n- Blame a person instead of the gap in the process\n:::\n\nKeep the client's channel updated in plain language throughout — \"we've identified it, customers are back to normal, root-cause fix landing shortly\" beats silence every time. Communication *is* incident response; see [standups and communication](/app/handbook).\n\n## Learn — blameless, always\n\n> An incident is a lesson the system paid for. Waste it by hunting for someone to blame and you'll pay for it twice.\n\nEvery incident of any size gets a short write-up: what happened, the timeline, the root cause, and — the only part that really matters — what change stops it recurring. A missing guard becomes a convention. A footgun becomes a Handbook warning. The person who was driving when it broke is exactly the person we want writing the fix, because they understand it best.\n\n::: check AFTER EVERY INCIDENT\n- Timeline written while it's fresh\n- Root cause named, not just the symptom\n- A concrete change that prevents a repeat\n- Handbook / conventions updated if needed\n- No blame — the process failed, not a person\n:::\n\n::: stats\n- Calm :: one driver, methodical\n- Stabilise first :: rollback beats a slow fix\n- Verify :: incident fixes still pass QA\n- Communicate :: the client hears it from us\n- Blameless :: fix the system, not the person\n\n:::\n",
  "keeping-a-branch-in-sync": "A feature branch drifts from `main` the moment someone else merges. Keeping it current is your job, and there's one right way to do it: **rebase onto `main`, never merge `main` into your branch.** Get this wrong and you pollute production's history with unreadable \"Merge branch main into feature/x\" commits — which was the single biggest source of deploy-feed noise before we cleaned it up.\n\n## The one command\n\nFetch the latest `main`, then replay your commits on top of it:\n\n```bash\ngit fetch origin\ngit rebase origin/main\n```\n\nThat's it. Your commits get lifted off, `main`'s new commits slot underneath, and yours land cleanly on top — as if you'd branched off the current `main` all along. The result is a linear history that squashes into one tidy commit at merge time.\n\nDo this regularly while a branch is open — daily on a long-lived branch — not just once at the end. Small, frequent rebases mean small, trivial conflicts. One giant rebase after a week means a conflict minefield.\n\n> Rebase to move your branch forward. Merge is for landing it on `main` — and even that we squash.\n\n## Never merge main in\n\nThe tempting-but-wrong move is `git merge main` (or `git pull` with the default merge strategy) while on your feature branch. It \"works\", but it creates a merge commit that carries `main`'s history *into* your branch — and if that ever reaches `main`, it lands as a garbage deploy line no one can read.\n\n::: avoid NEVER DO THIS\n- `git merge main` into your feature branch\n- `git pull` without `--rebase` on a feature branch\n- Push a \"Merge branch main into…\" commit\n- Rebase a branch someone else is working on\n- Force-push to a shared branch mid-review\n:::\n\n::: check DO THIS INSTEAD\n- `git fetch origin` then `git rebase origin/main`\n- `git pull --rebase` (or set it as the default)\n- Rebase early and often\n- Keep your branch yours until it's merged\n- Force-push *your own* branch after a rebase\n:::\n\nAfter a rebase, your branch has rewritten history, so a normal push is rejected. Push with lease — it's the safe force-push that refuses if someone else has touched the branch:\n\n```bash\ngit push --force-with-lease\n```\n\n## Resolving conflicts\n\nA rebase pauses on each conflicting commit. Work through them calmly:\n\n1. Open the conflicted files; resolve each `<<<<<<<` / `=======` / `>>>>>>>` marker, keeping the intent of *both* changes.\n2. `git add` the resolved files.\n3. `git rebase --continue` to move to the next commit.\n4. Repeat until the rebase finishes, then `git push --force-with-lease`.\n\nIf a file resolves to nothing to change, `git rebase --skip`. And if it all goes sideways, you are never trapped:\n\n```bash\ngit rebase --abort\n```\n\nThat returns you to exactly where you were before you started, no harm done. There's no such thing as a rebase you can't back out of.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> Conflicts are proportional to how far you've drifted. The best conflict-resolution strategy is rebasing often enough that there are almost none.\n\n## The Prisma schema gotcha\n\nOne recurring trap: `prisma/schema.prisma`. If both `main` and your branch touched the schema, you'll get a conflict, and it's easy to hand-resolve it into something malformed. After resolving *any* schema conflict, reformat and regenerate before you continue:\n\n```bash\nnpx prisma format\nnpx prisma generate\n```\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> A malformed schema won't just fail your build — remember `prisma db push` runs without `--accept-data-loss` and is all-or-nothing, so a botched merge can block the *entire* schema sync on deploy. Always `prisma format` after a schema conflict, and keep changes additive. See the [Prisma footgun](/app/handbook).\n\nOnce your branch is rebased, green, and conflict-free, it's ready for [review and squash-merge](/app/handbook). Keep it lean and keep it current — see [Git hygiene & repo health](/app/handbook) for keeping the wider repo tidy too.\n",
  "language-bash-shell": "Shell scripts are the glue around Foundry — deploy steps, database backups, cron jobs, seed and migration one-offs on the VPS. They run unattended, often as root, against production data, and a silent failure in a backup script is the kind of thing you only discover when you need the backup. So the bar for a shell script is the same as for anything else that touches production: fail loudly, quote everything, and know when the job has outgrown bash.\n\n## Safe defaults: `set -euo pipefail`\n\nBash's defaults are dangerous — it ploughs on after errors, treats unset variables as empty strings, and hides failures in the middle of a pipe. Turn that off at the top of every script.\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> Start every non-trivial script with `set -euo pipefail`. Without it, a failed `pg_dump` still exits 0, an undefined `$DIR` expands to nothing (hello `rm -rf /`), and a broken command mid-pipeline is invisible. This one line prevents the worst of it.\n\n```bash\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\nset -euo pipefail\n\n# -e  exit on any command failure\n# -u  error on unset variables\n# -o pipefail  a pipeline fails if ANY stage fails, not just the last\n```\n\n::: check WHAT THE FLAGS BUY YOU\n- `-e` — the script stops at the first error instead of charging on\n- `-u` — a typo'd or missing variable is caught, not silently empty\n- `-o pipefail` — `dump | gzip` fails if `dump` fails\n- Together — no silent partial success\n:::\n\n## Quote everything\n\nUnquoted variables are word-split and glob-expanded by the shell, so a path with a space, or a value containing `*`, does something you never intended. The rule has no exceptions: **quote every expansion**. Use `\"$var\"`, `\"${arr[@]}\"`, and `\"$(cmd)\"`.\n\n```bash\n# Wrong: breaks on spaces, expands globs, empty var becomes rm -rf /*\nrm -rf $BUILD_DIR/*\n\n# Right:\nrm -rf \"${BUILD_DIR:?BUILD_DIR must be set}\"/*\n\nbackup=\"/backups/foundry-$(date +%F).sql\"\npg_dump \"$DATABASE_URL\" > \"$backup\"\n```\n\nThe `${VAR:?message}` form is worth the habit for anything destructive — it aborts with your message if the variable is empty, so a missing env var can never turn a path into a wildcard.\n\n::: avoid SHELL FOOTGUNS\n- Unquoted `$var` in paths or arguments\n- `rm -rf $DIR/` with a possibly-empty `$DIR`\n- Parsing `ls` output — glob or use `find`\n- Assuming a command succeeded without checking\n- Secrets echoed into logs or passed as visible args\n- `cd somewhere &&` without handling the failure\n:::\n\n## Fail loudly, log clearly\n\nAn unattended script must announce what went wrong and exit non-zero so the caller (cron, GitHub Actions, systemd) knows. Send errors to stderr, exit with a real code, and don't bury failures in a `|| true` unless you genuinely mean \"this step is best-effort\".\n\n```bash\nlog()  { printf '[%s] %s\\n' \"$(date +%T)\" \"$*\"; }\ndie()  { printf 'ERROR: %s\\n' \"$*\" >&2; exit 1; }\n\ncommand -v pg_dump >/dev/null || die \"pg_dump not installed\"\nlog \"backing up to $backup\"\npg_dump \"$DATABASE_URL\" > \"$backup\" || die \"backup failed\"\nlog \"done\"\n```\n\nOur cron jobs on the VPS (`meet-transcripts`, `pulse-reconcile`, `docs-gdrive-backup`) are triggered this way — guarded, logged, and loud when they break.\n\n> A shell script that fails quietly is worse than no script at all — it lets you believe the work happened.\n\n## Know when to stop\n\nBash is superb for wiring commands together and hopeless at anything with real logic. The moment you're reaching for arrays of structured data, nested conditionals, string parsing or arithmetic that matters, you've outgrown it — rewrite it as a Node or TypeScript script (we keep one-offs in `scripts/`). The maintenance and correctness win is enormous.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> Rule of thumb: more than ~30 lines, any real data structure, or logic you'd want to unit-test → make it a `.mjs`/`.ts` script instead. `scripts/parse-clickup-csv.mjs` is exactly this call — CSV parsing was never a job for bash.\n\n## House rules\n\nTest destructive scripts with `echo` in front of the dangerous command first, or a `--dry-run` guard. Never commit secrets — read them from the VPS `.env`, never hardcode. And prefer `#!/usr/bin/env bash` over `#!/bin/bash` for portability.\n\n::: stats\n- `set -euo pipefail` :: on every script\n- Quoted :: every single expansion\n- Loud :: non-zero exit, clear error\n- Bash stays small :: outgrow it, rewrite it\n:::\n",
  "language-css-tailwind": "We style Foundry with Tailwind CSS v4 — CSS-first, with no `tailwind.config.js`; the whole configuration lives in `globals.css` under `@layer` rules. Tailwind gives you speed, but speed without discipline produces a hundred bespoke shades of nearly-blue and text that vanishes in dark mode. The rule that keeps it coherent is short: style with our design tokens, never with raw values. Read this alongside [building on the design system](/app/handbook) — this guide is the mechanics, that one is the intent.\n\n## Tokens, not hex\n\nColours, and anything else that must respond to theme, come from CSS variables — `--text-*`, `--surface-*`, `--border-*`, `--brand-*`. A hardcoded `#1D4ED8` or `bg-white` is frozen to one theme and drifts from the system the moment the palette moves. Tokens are the single source of truth.\n\n```html\n<!-- Good: reads the system, adapts to dark mode -->\n<div class=\"bg-[var(--surface-card)] text-[var(--text-primary)] border border-[var(--border-subtle)]\">\n  <span class=\"text-[var(--brand-blue)]\">01 // PULSE</span>\n</div>\n\n<!-- Wrong: frozen values, invisible in dark mode -->\n<div class=\"bg-white text-slate-900 border-gray-200\">…</div>\n```\n\nGitwork Blue (`#1D4ED8`) is the only accent — there is no second colour outside the blue family. If a design seems to need one, it doesn't; it needs hierarchy.\n\n## The dark-mode invisible-text trap\n\nThis is the single most common styling bug we ship. Our dark-mode remap covers `bg-white`, the semantic tokens and rgba-black borders — but it does **not** remap Tailwind's `neutral`/`slate`/`zinc`/`gray`/`stone` scales. So `text-slate-700` on a navy shell in dark mode is dark text on a dark background: invisible.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Never use `text-gray-*`, `bg-slate-*`, `border-zinc-*` and friends for anything that must survive dark mode. Use the `--text-*` / `--surface-*` / `--border-*` tokens. If you can't read it after toggling the theme, this is why.\n\n::: avoid COLOUR CLASSES THAT BREAK DARK MODE\n- `text-slate-*`, `text-gray-*`, `text-zinc-*`\n- `bg-neutral-*`, `bg-stone-*`\n- `border-gray-*`, `border-zinc-*`\n- Any raw `#hex` or `rgb()` in a class\n:::\n\n::: check USE THESE INSTEAD\n- `text-[var(--text-primary)]` / `--text-muted`\n- `bg-[var(--surface-canvas)]` / `--surface-card`\n- `border-[var(--border-subtle)]`\n- `text-[var(--brand-blue)]` for the one accent\n:::\n\n## Geometry and the no-pill rule\n\nOur shapes are deliberate and tight: **6px** radius on controls (buttons, inputs), **10px** on cards. No fully-rounded pills anywhere — the only exception is a **6px status dot**. The canvas is a warm cream, never pure white. Keep to those and new UI drops straight into the system.\n\n> If it's a pill, it's wrong. The only round thing we ship is a 6px status dot.\n\nThe widget signature is part of the geometry too: numbered mono headers like `01 // NAME` in JetBrains Mono. Match it and your panel belongs; invent your own header and it stands out for the wrong reason.\n\n## Layout, responsive and merging classes\n\nBuild layouts with flexbox and grid, size with relative units, and design mobile-up. Responsive is a whole-system concern, not a per-screen patch — see [responsive & mobile standards](/app/handbook) before you sprinkle breakpoints.\n\nFor conditional and merged class strings, use `cn()` from `src/lib/format.ts` rather than template-string concatenation — it dedupes and resolves Tailwind conflicts cleanly.\n\n```tsx\nimport { cn } from \"@/lib/format\";\n\n<button\n  className={cn(\n    \"rounded-[6px] px-3 py-1.5 text-sm\",\n    \"bg-[var(--brand-blue)] text-white\",\n    disabled && \"opacity-50 pointer-events-none\",\n  )}\n/>\n```\n\n> [!TIP]\n> One more Tailwind v4 gotcha: the anchor reset **must** live inside `@layer base` in `globals.css`. Unlayered CSS beats utility layers in the v4 cascade, so an out-of-layer `a { color }` will silently override your `text-*` classes.\n\n## House rules\n\n::: stats\n- Tokens :: never hardcoded hex\n- Dark-mode safe :: no neutral/slate/gray scales\n- 6px controls · 10px cards :: no pills\n- One accent :: Gitwork Blue\n- `cn()` :: for every conditional class\n:::\n",
  "language-javascript-node": "JavaScript is the runtime underneath everything we ship — the Node server that runs Foundry, the scripts that seed and migrate, the code your TypeScript compiles down to. TypeScript catches shape errors; it does nothing about the language's genuinely sharp edges. This guide is about writing JS that behaves the way you think it does, especially around async and coercion.\n\n## Do async in parallel by default\n\nThe single most common performance bug we see is awaiting things in a loop that have no reason to be sequential. If three fetches don't depend on each other, run them together. Sequential `await` turns 3×200ms into 600ms for no reason.\n\n```js\n// Slow: each await blocks the next, for nothing.\nfor (const id of ids) {\n  results.push(await fetchClient(id));\n}\n\n// Fast: all in flight at once.\nconst results = await Promise.all(ids.map((id) => fetchClient(id)));\n\n// When one failing shouldn't sink the rest (best-effort side-effects):\nconst settled = await Promise.allSettled(notify.map(send));\n```\n\nReserve sequential `await` for genuine data dependencies (B needs A's result). Everything else is `Promise.all`. This is exactly how our shared scan core fans out its category modules.\n\n::: check ASYNC DONE RIGHT\n- Independent work → `Promise.all`\n- Best-effort work → `Promise.allSettled`\n- Every promise `await`ed or explicitly `.catch()`ed\n- `async` functions that can reject are wrapped where they're called\n- No floating promises (the lint rule is on for a reason)\n:::\n\n## Know the falsy sharp edges\n\nJavaScript's coercion rules are a minefield, and the mines are all around empty values. `0`, `\"\"` and `false` are falsy — so `if (count)` skips zero, and `value || fallback` clobbers a legitimate empty string or zero. Use `??` (nullish coalescing) when you mean \"only if null or undefined\".\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> `retainerDays || 25` returns 25 when the real value is `0`. `retainerDays ?? 25` returns 25 only when it's null/undefined. Reach for `??` and `?.` unless you *specifically* want the falsy-catch behaviour of `||`.\n\n```js\nconst days = client.retainerDays ?? 25;   // 0 survives\nconst name = user.name?.trim() ?? \"\";      // no throw on missing user\nif (items.length === 0) return empty();    // explicit, not `if (!items.length)` guesswork\n```\n\n## `===` always, and other modern idioms\n\n`==` does implicit coercion (`0 == \"\"`, `null == undefined`, `[] == false` are all true) and there is no situation where its surprises are worth it. Use `===` and `!==`, full stop. Beyond that, lean on the modern language — it's clearer and it's what everyone reading the code expects.\n\n::: avoid LEGACY JS WE DON'T WRITE\n- `==` / `!=` (loose equality)\n- `var` — use `const`, and `let` only when you reassign\n- `for (var i…)` index loops where `.map`/`.filter`/`.find` read cleaner\n- Mutating function arguments or shared arrays in place\n- `arguments` object — use rest params `(...args)`\n:::\n\n```js\n// Prefer expressive, immutable transforms:\nconst { id, ...rest } = record;                    // destructure\nconst merged = { ...defaults, ...overrides };      // spread, don't mutate\nconst total = lines.reduce((sum, l) => sum + l.amount, 0);\nconst byId = new Map(rows.map((r) => [r.id, r]));   // Map for lookups, not {} bags\n```\n\n## Dependency discipline\n\nEvery dependency is code you now maintain, a supply-chain surface, and weight in the Docker image. Before you `npm install`, ask whether the platform already does it. We deliberately hand-rolled markdown rendering, the toast system and the Gantt chart rather than pull libraries — smaller, safer, no version churn.\n\n> Every dependency is a liability you took on to avoid writing ten lines. Count the cost both ways.\n\n::: check BEFORE ADDING A DEPENDENCY\n- Can the standard library or an existing helper do it?\n- Is it actively maintained and reasonably small?\n- Does it bloat the server bundle or image?\n- Would ~20 lines of our own be clearer and safer?\n:::\n\n## House rules\n\nNode is a long-running process on the VPS, not a per-request lambda — an unhandled rejection or a leaked timer stays around. Handle every error path, don't block the event loop with sync CPU work, and let failures surface loudly rather than swallowing them into a silent `catch {}`.\n\n::: stats\n- Parallel by default :: sequential only on real dependencies\n- `??` and `?.` :: not `||` over falsy values\n- `===` always :: no loose coercion\n- Few deps :: each one earns its place\n:::\n",
  "language-sql": "We reach SQL mostly through Prisma, but Prisma is a thin wrapper over a real PostgreSQL database — and the database is where Foundry lives or dies under load. Every query you write runs against production data on the VPS, so the discipline here is about making Postgres do the work: index what you filter, fetch in one round trip, and aggregate in the engine rather than in Node. Learn the shapes below and you'll avoid the handful of query mistakes that cause almost all our slow pages.\n\n## Index what you filter, order or join on\n\nAn unindexed `WHERE` on a growing table means a full sequential scan — fine on ten rows, a disaster on a hundred thousand. If you routinely filter, sort or join on a column, it needs an index. In Prisma that's `@@index` (or `@unique` when it's also a constraint).\n\n```prisma\nmodel Task {\n  id            String  @id @default(cuid())\n  workspaceId   String\n  clientId      String\n  status        TaskStatus\n  featureBlockId String?\n\n  @@index([workspaceId, clientId])   // how we always list tasks\n  @@index([status])\n}\n```\n\nCompound indexes match the order you filter in: `[workspaceId, clientId]` serves both a workspace-wide and a per-client query. Add the index in the same PR as the query that needs it — and keep the change additive (see the [Prisma db-push footgun](/app/handbook)).\n\n## Kill the N+1\n\nThe classic killer: fetch a list, then loop and query once per row. A hundred clients becomes a hundred and one queries. Prisma's `include`/`select` pulls the related rows in a single round trip — use it.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> If you find yourself `await`ing a query inside a `.map` or `for` loop over database rows, stop — that's an N+1. Pull the relations with `include`, or batch the ids into one `where: { id: { in: ids } }`. Our `client-metrics.ts` was written specifically to batch across the whole client set with no per-row queries.\n\n```ts\n// N+1: one query for clients, then one per client. Don't.\nconst clients = await prisma.workspaceClient.findMany({ where: { workspaceId } });\nfor (const c of clients) {\n  c.placements = await prisma.placement.findMany({ where: { clientId: c.id } });\n}\n\n// One round trip.\nconst clients = await prisma.workspaceClient.findMany({\n  where: { workspaceId },\n  include: { placements: { where: { endDate: null } } },\n});\n```\n\n## Let the database do the maths\n\nDon't pull ten thousand rows into Node to count or sum them — Postgres does aggregation faster and ships far less over the wire. Use `groupBy`, `count`, `aggregate`.\n\n```ts\n// Count active devs per client in the engine, not with .length in JS.\nconst counts = await prisma.placement.groupBy({\n  by: [\"clientId\"],\n  where: { endDate: null },\n  _count: { candidateId: true },\n});\n```\n\n::: check MAKE POSTGRES EARN ITS KEEP\n- Filter, sort, paginate in the query — not in JS\n- `select` only the columns you use (don't `SELECT *` a wide row)\n- Aggregate with `groupBy`/`count`/`aggregate`\n- Scope every query to the workspace\n- `take`/`skip` for pagination on large tables\n:::\n\n## Select only what you use\n\nEvery column you fetch is bytes serialised, sent and parsed. Wide rows (documents with big JSON blobs, receipts as `bytea`) are expensive to haul around wholesale. Name the fields you need with `select`, especially in list endpoints.\n\n> A query that fetches everything \"just in case\" pays for that insurance on every single request.\n\n## pgvector: semantic search\n\nPostgres carries the `pgvector` extension (created on boot in `bootstrap.ts`), which powers Care's semantic search over conversations via an `embedding vector(1536)` column with an HNSW index. When you add vector search elsewhere, follow that pattern: store the embedding alongside the row, index it for approximate nearest-neighbour, and query by cosine distance — don't invent a parallel store.\n\n## House rules\n\n::: avoid QUERY ANTI-PATTERNS\n- N+1 loops over database rows\n- `SELECT *` on wide tables in hot paths\n- Counting/summing in JS instead of the engine\n- Unindexed filters on growing tables\n- Unbounded `findMany` with no `take`\n- Forgetting the `workspaceId` scope\n:::\n\nSee [data & prisma conventions](/app/handbook) for the singleton client, DTO boundaries and the schema-change rules that sit on top of all this.\n\n::: stats\n- Indexed :: what you filter and join on\n- One round trip :: no N+1\n- Aggregated in Postgres :: not in Node\n- Scoped :: always to the workspace\n:::\n",
  "language-swift-ios": "The Foundry iOS app is a native SwiftUI companion that talks to the *same* Foundry API the web app does — same endpoints, same auth, same DTOs. That single fact governs almost every decision here: the phone is a thin, beautiful client over a backend that already owns the truth. Write Swift that leans on the compiler, embraces value types, and mirrors the platform's design so the two feel like one product.\n\n## SwiftUI-first, value types by default\n\nBuild screens declaratively in SwiftUI. Model your data as `struct`s and `enum`s — value types are copied, not shared, so a whole category of aliasing and threading bugs simply can't happen. Reach for a `class` only when you genuinely need reference semantics or observation (`@Observable` view models, long-lived stores).\n\n```swift\nstruct Client: Identifiable, Decodable {\n    let id: String\n    let name: String\n    let devCount: Int\n    let monthlyCost: Money?   // optional mirrors the API: gated fields may be absent\n}\n\nenum LoadState<Value> {\n    case loading\n    case failed(String)\n    case loaded(Value)\n}\n```\n\nThat `LoadState` enum is the Swift twin of our TypeScript discriminated unions — model every screen as loading / failed / loaded and the impossible in-between states can't be drawn. See [language-typescript](/app/handbook) for the same idea on the web.\n\n::: check EVERY SCREEN HANDLES\n- A loading state\n- An empty state (no data, not an error)\n- An error state with a retry\n- Dynamic Type + VoiceOver labels\n- Dark mode (the app ships both)\n:::\n\n## Concurrency with async/await, not callbacks\n\nNetworking is `async/await` over `URLSession`. No completion-handler pyramids, no `DispatchQueue.main.async` sprinkled to fix threading — mark UI-touching types `@MainActor` and let structured concurrency place the work correctly.\n\n```swift\n@MainActor\nfinal class ClientListModel: ObservableObject {\n    @Published var state: LoadState<[Client]> = .loading\n\n    func load() async {\n        state = .loading\n        do {\n            state = .loaded(try await api.get(\"/api/clients\", as: [Client].self))\n        } catch {\n            state = .failed(error.localizedDescription)\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\nRun independent calls concurrently with `async let` or a task group — the same \"don't await sequentially for nothing\" rule as [Node](/app/handbook).\n\n```swift\nasync let clients = api.get(\"/api/clients\", as: [Client].self)\nasync let meetings = api.get(\"/api/desk/action-items\", as: [ActionItem].self)\nlet (c, m) = try await (clients, meetings)\n```\n\n## The API is the contract\n\nThe app is mobile-JWT-aware: endpoints authenticate via `requireAuthedUser()` server-side, and the same route serves web and iOS. That means the phone must never re-implement business logic the server owns — costing, permission gating, scoping to a workspace. If the API decides a developer can't see client financials, the app shows what it's given; it does not compute the number itself.\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> The server is the source of truth. If the app and the API disagree, the API wins — and the fix usually belongs on the server, so web and iOS both get it. Decode DTOs, don't reconstruct logic.\n\n::: avoid DON'T DO THIS ON THE CLIENT\n- Recompute costs, permissions, or scoping the API already gates\n- Trust a field the server withholds by role\n- Hardcode an endpoint the web app resolves dynamically\n- Ship a struct that silently diverges from the DTO\n:::\n\n## Design parity with the platform\n\nFoundry has one visual language (`DESIGN.md`) and the app honours it: Gitwork Blue as the only accent, the Inter / DM Serif Display / JetBrains Mono type triple, cream surfaces rather than pure white, tight geometry and no gratuitous pills. The On Your Desk drawer, the dashboards and the standup all exist on both platforms and should read as the same product on either.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> Before building a screen, find its web equivalent and match the hierarchy, the labels and the mono/serif roles. Parity is a feature — a client should never feel they've switched apps. See [building on the design system](/app/handbook).\n\n## House rules\n\nLet the compiler help you: prefer `let` over `var`, unwrap optionals with `if let` / `guard let` (never force-unwrap `!` outside tests or provable invariants), and keep view bodies small by extracting subviews. Verify against a real device size and Dynamic Type before you call it done.\n\n::: stats\n- SwiftUI + value types :: bugs designed out\n- async/await :: no callback pyramids\n- API owns the truth :: the app renders it\n- Design parity :: one product, two platforms\n:::\n",
  "language-typescript": "TypeScript is the language the whole of Foundry is written in, and we treat its type system as a design tool, not a formality. Good types make wrong states unrepresentable and let you refactor fearlessly; bad types are decoration that lies. The house rule is simple: type the boundaries hard, and let inference do the middle.\n\n## Type the boundaries, infer the middle\n\nAnnotate what crosses a line — function parameters, return types on exported functions, the shape of anything coming off the wire. Inside a function, let TypeScript work it out. Over-annotating locals just adds noise that drifts out of date.\n\n```ts\n// Good: the boundary is explicit, the body is inferred.\nexport function serialiseClient(row: WorkspaceClient): ClientDto {\n  const activeDevs = row.placements.filter((p) => p.endDate === null);\n  return { id: row.id, name: row.name, devCount: activeDevs.length };\n}\n\n// Noise: every local re-stated for no gain.\nconst activeDevs: Placement[] = row.placements.filter((p: Placement): boolean => ...);\n```\n\nKeep our DTOs separate from Prisma row types — the row is a database shape, the DTO is the contract your API and iOS app depend on. See [data & prisma conventions](/app/handbook).\n\n## `any` is the enemy\n\n`any` switches the type-checker off for everything it touches, and it spreads. One `any` in a return type silently un-types every caller downstream. If you genuinely don't know a shape, reach for `unknown` and narrow it — that forces you to prove the shape before you use it.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> `any` is a hole in the hull, not a shortcut. We carry ~135 legacy `any`s as tracked debt; do not add to the pile. If you're fighting a type, the answer is a better type or `unknown` + a guard — never `any`.\n\n::: avoid REACH FOR THESE INSTEAD OF `any`\n- `unknown` + a narrowing guard for genuinely untyped input\n- A discriminated union for \"one of these shapes\"\n- `Parameters<>` / `ReturnType<>` to derive, not restate\n- A Zod schema's inferred type at the true edge\n:::\n\n## Model states as unions, not optional-soup\n\nA bag of optional booleans lets you express nonsense — `isLoading && isError && data`. A discriminated union makes the impossible states unrepresentable and gives you exhaustive `switch` checks for free.\n\n```ts\n// Optional-soup: four booleans, sixteen states, most invalid.\ntype Scan = { loading?: boolean; error?: string; data?: Result; done?: boolean };\n\n// Union: exactly the states that can happen.\ntype Scan =\n  | { status: \"running\"; progress: number }\n  | { status: \"failed\"; error: string }\n  | { status: \"complete\"; result: Result };\n\nfunction label(scan: Scan) {\n  switch (scan.status) {\n    case \"running\": return `${scan.progress}%`;\n    case \"failed\": return scan.error;\n    case \"complete\": return \"Done\";\n    // no default → adding a new status is a compile error until handled\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThat last point is the payoff: our Pulse check categories are a typed union for exactly this reason — a typo'd category is a build failure, not a runtime surprise.\n\n## Validate at the edge with Zod\n\nTypeScript types are erased at runtime. Anything you didn't write — request bodies, external API responses, webhook payloads — is `unknown` until proven otherwise. Parse it with a Zod schema at the boundary and let the *inferred* type flow inward.\n\n> A type you asserted is a hope. A type you parsed is a fact.\n\n```ts\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\n\nconst bodySchema = z.object({ name: z.string().min(1), retainerDays: z.number().int().min(0).max(31) });\ntype ClientInput = z.infer<typeof bodySchema>; // one source of truth\n\nconst parsed = bodySchema.parse(await req.json()); // throws → caught by fromError\n```\n\nAll our schemas live in `src/server/validators.ts`. Never hand-write a `type` and a validator that can drift apart — infer one from the other.\n\n## House rules\n\n::: check EVERY TS FILE\n- Boundaries typed, locals inferred\n- `unknown` + guard instead of `any`\n- Unions over optional booleans for state\n- Zod-parse anything off the wire\n- Return types on exported functions\n- No `@ts-ignore` without a one-line why\n:::\n\nThe linter is not your adversary; it's the cheapest reviewer you have. If `npm run lint` and `tsc` are clean, you've cleared the bar that catches most of what a human reviewer would otherwise waste time on. See [code style & conventions](/app/handbook) and [API route conventions](/app/handbook) for how these types meet our routes.\n\n::: stats\n- Boundaries typed :: the middle inferred\n- No new `any` :: `unknown` and narrow\n- Unions :: impossible states unrepresentable\n- Parsed at the edge :: not merely asserted\n:::\n",
  "local-environment-setup": "Getting Foundry running locally is a fifteen-minute job if you do it in order, and a frustrating afternoon if you don't. Foundry is a Next.js 15 app on React 19 and TypeScript, backed by Prisma over PostgreSQL. This is the exact path from a fresh clone to a booting app, plus how to verify your work when the browser can't help you.\n\nDo it once, do it properly, and you won't think about it again. The two things that trip people up are the database URLs and the encryption key — both are called out below.\n\n## Prerequisites\n\nHave these installed before you start. Versions matter less than being current, but Node must be recent enough for Next 15.\n\n::: check BEFORE YOU BEGIN\n- Node.js (a current LTS) and npm\n- Git, with access to `Git-Dann/docs-by-gitwork`\n- A PostgreSQL you can reach (local Docker or a dev instance)\n- An Anthropic API key for the AI features\n- Your editor of choice with TypeScript support\n:::\n\n## Clone and install\n\nStandard from here. Clone, install, generate the Prisma client.\n\n```bash\ngit clone https://github.com/Git-Dann/docs-by-gitwork.git\ncd docs-by-gitwork\nnpm install\nnpm run db:generate   # prisma generate — creates the typed client\n```\n\n## Environment variables\n\nCreate a `.env.local` in the project root. Production secrets live in the VPS `.env` and never in git — for local dev you supply your own. These are the ones you'll actually need to boot and work:\n\n| Variable | What it's for |\n|---|---|\n| `DATABASE_URL` | Postgres connection (pooled) — app reads/writes through this |\n| `DIRECT_URL` | Direct Postgres connection — Prisma uses it for schema pushes |\n| `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` | Default AI provider for Pulse, Study, Scribe etc. |\n| `API_KEY` | Gates every `/api/` route except `/api/health` |\n| `ENCRYPTION_KEY` | 32-byte base64 secret — encrypts onboarding bank details at rest |\n| `GITHUB_TOKEN` | Repo read + PR creation for Pulse scans and the fix-agent |\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> Two things bite here. First, `DATABASE_URL` and `DIRECT_URL` are both required — Prisma won't push schema without the direct one. Second, generate `ENCRYPTION_KEY` once with `openssl rand -base64 32` and never change it: if it moves, any encrypted data becomes unreadable. Keep your local value stable.\n\nPush the schema to your database once the URLs are set:\n\n```bash\nnpm run db:push   # prisma db push — syncs schema.prisma to your DB\n```\n\n## The commands you'll live in\n\nYou'll type these dozens of times a day. Learn them now.\n\n```bash\nnpm run dev          # dev server at localhost:3000\nnpm run build        # prisma generate → db push → next build (the full check)\nnpm run lint         # ESLint — must be clean before you push\nnpm run db:generate  # regenerate the Prisma client after schema edits\nnpm run db:push      # push schema changes to your DB\n```\n\n## Verifying without the browser\n\nHere's the important bit: **the app is auth-gated and there's no seeded local dataset**, so you often can't just click through a change to confirm it. Lean on the compiler and linter instead — they catch the majority of mistakes before a human ever would.\n\n::: check HOW WE VERIFY LOCALLY\n- `npx tsc --noEmit` — types check across the whole app\n- `npm run lint` — no ESLint errors or warnings\n- `npm run build` — the real gate; if this passes, CI will too\n- Read the diff back yourself before opening a PR\n:::\n\nRun `tsc` and `lint` as your default proof of correctness. A change that compiles, lints, and builds cleanly is in good shape even if you never rendered it.\n\n## When it won't boot\n\nMost first-run failures fall into a short list. Work through these before asking for help — then ask, the same day.\n\n::: avoid COMMON SELF-INFLICTED WOUNDS\n- Missing `DIRECT_URL` (db push silently can't run)\n- Stale Prisma client after a schema edit (re-run `db:generate`)\n- Reusing a prod `ENCRYPTION_KEY` you don't actually have\n- Committing `.env.local` — it's git-ignored, keep it that way\n- Editing `schema.prisma` with a data-losing change (see the [Prisma footgun](/app/handbook))\n:::\n\n> [!TIP]\n> If the build fails on Prisma, it's almost always the schema or a URL — not your code. Check `db:generate` ran and both URLs point somewhere real before you go hunting elsewhere.\n\nOnce `npm run dev` serves `localhost:3000` and `npm run build` passes, you're ready. Next, work through the [first-week checklist](/app/handbook) to ship your first change, and skim [code style and conventions](/app/handbook) so your first PR lands clean.\n",
  "release-process": "No feature, fix or UI change reaches customers without completing this process. Production is a live environment with real customer data — we treat it like one, every time. This is the path every change walks from a developer's branch to a customer's screen, and the gates it must pass on the way.\n\n## Why this exists\n\nTwo environments, two jobs. Keeping them honest is what lets us ship fast without shipping regressions.\n\n::: grid\n### A live customer environment\nProduction is used by real customers with real business data. It must stay stable, clean and reliable at all times. It is **never** an extension of development.\n### Where we prove it works\nStaging is a dedicated environment to develop, test, validate and refine features before release. Every change is validated here first, without exception.\n:::\n\n## The release workflow\n\nEvery change moves left to right. Nothing skips a step; product review happens where required.\n\n::: steps\n- Development\n- Developer testing\n- Deploy to staging\n- QA testing\n- Product review\n- Deploy to production\n:::\n\n## Developer testing\n\nValidate your own work before it moves to QA. If you haven't tested it, it isn't ready.\n\n::: check EVERY CHANGE INCLUDES\n- Happy-path testing\n- Edge-case testing\n- All forms validated\n- Success & error notifications\n- Roles & permissions checked\n- Responsive layout checks\n- Zero console errors\n- Debug code & test data removed\n:::\n\n## Staging environment\n\nOur dedicated testing environment. All new functionality is validated here before release — features, fixes, UI changes, integrations and permissions.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> Test users, dummy warranty claims, sample images, fake emails and experimental functionality are acceptable in staging only. None of it may ever reach Production.\n\n## QA testing\n\nQA validates that work is production-ready. Only once QA signs off does work move towards Production.\n\n::: grid\n### Functional\n- Behaves as expected\n- Existing functionality still works\n- Edge cases handled correctly\n- Validation messages display correctly\n### UI & UX\n- Layout correct & responsive\n- Consistent spacing & styling\n- British English throughout\n- Notifications behave correctly\n### Data validation\n- Records save correctly\n- Relationships maintained\n- Statuses update correctly\n- No duplicate records\n### Performance\n- Pages load correctly\n- No unnecessary delays\n- No obvious performance issues\n:::\n\n## Production\n\nConfirm the checklist before deploying. Production always represents the highest-quality version of the product.\n\n::: check BEFORE DEPLOYING, CONFIRM\n- No test data exists\n- No dummy accounts remain\n- No placeholder content\n- No debug information visible\n- Feature flags reviewed\n- Database migrations verified\n- Rollback plan available if required\n- No incomplete functionality accessible\n:::\n\n::: avoid NEVER IN PRODUCTION\n- Testing with dummy data\n- Creating fake suppliers or customers\n- Creating test warranty claims\n- Leaving placeholder content\n- Leaving development images\n- Deploying unfinished features\n- Deploying work that skipped QA\n:::\n\n> If a feature cannot be safely tested in Production, stop and raise it with Product before proceeding.\n\n## Release sign-off\n\nBefore every deployment, the developer confirms — out loud, in the channel — that all of this is true.\n\n::: pills RELEASE SIGN-OFF\n- Development complete\n- Self-testing complete\n- QA approved the release\n- No critical known issues\n- Ready for customer use\n:::\n\n## Why this process matters\n\nRecent releases have shown what happens without it. Every one of these is avoidable when we follow Staging → QA → Production:\n\n- Test data appearing in Production\n- Dummy suppliers & placeholder content visible to customers\n- Bugs reaching customers\n- Features behaving differently in Production than expected\n\nOur goal is simple: customers should only ever see polished, production-ready software. Every deployment leaves Production ready for customers.\n\n::: stats\n- Stable\n- Clean\n- Free from test data\n- Free from unfinished work\n- Fully tested\n- Ready for customers\n:::\n",
  "responsive-and-mobile-standards": "Responsive work is a whole-system discipline, not a per-screen patch. The failure mode we keep out of Foundry is the \"fix\" that makes one page look right on a phone while quietly breaking the shared component it borrowed from — and three other screens with it. Before you touch any layout, read the [mobile playbook](/app/handbook); this is the short version of the operating standard.\n\n## Fix the primitive, not the page\n\nMost layout bugs live in a shared component, not the screen you noticed them on. If a card overflows on mobile, the card is wrong everywhere — patching it inside one page just hides the symptom and forks the component's behaviour.\n\n> If your responsive fix only touches one screen, you probably fixed the symptom, not the bug.\n\n::: steps\n- Reproduce at a real width\n- Find the shared component behind it\n- Fix it there, once\n- Check every screen that uses it\n:::\n\n## The breakpoints\n\nTailwind's defaults, used consistently. Design mobile-first: unprefixed classes are the phone, and each breakpoint *adds* to layouts you scale up from there.\n\n| Prefix | Min width | Typically |\n|---|---|---|\n| (none) | 0 | Phone — the base layout |\n| `sm` | 640px | Large phone |\n| `md` | 768px | Tablet |\n| `lg` | 1024px | Small laptop |\n| `xl` | 1280px | Desktop (e.g. split-screen preview) |\n\nBuild the narrow layout first and let it grow. Retrofitting mobile onto a desktop-only layout is where the worst blast-radius bugs come from.\n\n## Rules that prevent the common bugs\n\nThese are the handful of habits that stop the recurring mobile regressions before they ship.\n\n::: check RESPONSIVE RULES\n- Mobile-first: base styles are the phone\n- Fix shared components, not individual screens\n- Flex/grid that wraps, not fixed pixel widths\n- Wide content (tables, Gantt, code) scrolls in its own `overflow-x` container\n- Tap targets comfortably large\n- Test the app-shell nav, drawer and modals at every width\n:::\n\n::: avoid DON'T\n- Silo a fix to the one screen you noticed\n- Let the page body scroll horizontally\n- Assume desktop-only layouts \"probably work\" on mobile\n- Hard-code widths that can't shrink\n:::\n\nThe horizontal-scroll rule matters most. The page body must never scroll sideways — wide things (data tables, the Gantt chart, long code blocks) get wrapped in their own `overflow-x: auto` container so they scroll independently while the layout around them stays put.\n\n```tsx\n// Wide content scrolls itself; the page never does\n<div className=\"overflow-x-auto\">\n  <table className=\"min-w-[720px]\">…</table>\n</div>\n```\n\n## Verify at real widths\n\nYou cannot eyeball responsiveness from a desktop window. The bugs live at 375px, in the app-shell sidebar collapsing, in a modal that's fine on a laptop and unusable on a phone.\n\n::: check BEFORE YOU CALL IT DONE\n- Checked at ~375px (phone)\n- Checked at tablet and desktop\n- Nav / Desk drawer / modals all behave\n- No horizontal body scroll anywhere\n- Nothing regressed on sibling screens\n:::\n\nFoundry also has a native iOS companion built in SwiftUI that talks to the same API. Web responsiveness and the native app are separate surfaces, but they share design intent — a component that behaves predictably on the web is easier to keep in parity with its native counterpart. Keep both in mind when you change a shared layout pattern, and lean on the tokens and primitives from [building on the design system](/app/handbook) so the change lands consistently.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> Reproduce at the real breakpoint first, fix the shared component, then sweep every screen that uses it. That order is the whole discipline.\n\n::: stats\n- Whole system :: fix primitives, not pages\n- Mobile-first :: base is the phone, scale up\n- No sideways scroll :: body never, wide content self-scrolls\n- Real widths :: verify at 375px, not a resized laptop\n- Blast radius :: check every screen the change touches\n\n:::\n",
  "scoping-and-estimation": "Most estimates are wrong because they answer the wrong question. Someone asks \"how long will this take?\" and you picture yourself writing the happy-path code — not the review, not the QA, not the deploy, not the two rounds of fixes after the client sees it. Scoping and estimation is the craft of committing to a number you can actually hit, and being honest about how uncertain it is. Get this right and everything downstream — client trust, standups, planning — gets easier.\n\n## Scope first, estimate second\n\nYou cannot estimate what you haven't scoped. Before any number comes out of your mouth, you should be able to write down what \"done\" means for this piece of work — the states, the edge cases, the permissions, the responsive behaviour. If you can't, that's not an estimate problem, it's a scoping gap, and the honest answer is \"let me scope it and come back.\"\n\n::: steps\n- Understand the ask\n- Write down \"done\"\n- Break into tasks\n- Estimate the whole thing\n- Commit & track\n:::\n\n::: check A SCOPE IS READY WHEN YOU KNOW\n- What \"done\" looks like, concretely\n- The states: loading, empty, error, success\n- Which roles & permissions are involved\n- What data changes and whether it's additive\n- What's explicitly *out* of scope\n:::\n\nWriting down what's out of scope is half the battle. Unspoken assumptions are where projects quietly double in size.\n\n## Estimate the whole thing, not the code\n\nThe code is often the smallest part. A change isn't finished when it compiles — it's finished when it's live, verified, and the client's happy. Estimate every phase, not just the fun bit.\n\n```\nReal estimate = build\n              + self-testing (happy path + edge cases)\n              + code review + addressing feedback\n              + QA\n              + deploy + watching it land\n              + the fixes after the client sees it\n```\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> Schema changes carry a hidden tax. Because `prisma db push` runs without `--accept-data-loss` and its safety check is all-or-nothing, anything non-additive needs extra care and often a manual step — see [database changes and the Prisma footgun](/app/handbook). If your work touches the schema, add time for it and keep the diff additive.\n\n## Ranges beat false precision\n\n\"Three days\" sounds confident and is almost always a lie. A range tells the truth: it encodes how much you don't yet know. The wider the range, the more scoping is still owed.\n\n| You say | It reads as | Use when |\n|---|---|---|\n| \"Half a day\" | High confidence, well-trodden | You've done this exact thing before |\n| \"2–4 days\" | Real work, some unknowns | Normal feature, scope is clear |\n| \"1–2 weeks, needs a spike\" | Genuine uncertainty | New integration, unclear data shape |\n| \"Can't estimate yet\" | Honest | Scope isn't written down |\n\n> A precise number you'll miss is worth less than a range you'll hit.\n\nTie the range to risk out loud: \"2–4 days, closer to 4 if their API pagination is as bad as it looks.\" That single sentence does more for client trust than any padded single figure.\n\n## Commit and track\n\nOnce you've given a number, it becomes a promise — so track it against reality. Break the work into tasks on the [Portal](/app/handbook) board and let the daily [standups](/app/handbook) show movement. The moment the estimate is at risk, say so; a slip named early is a normal update, a slip discovered late is a broken promise.\n\n::: avoid NEVER\n- Estimate to please, then miss quietly\n- Quote a single number for genuinely unknown work\n- Pad estimates instead of naming the risk\n- Forget review, QA, deploy and fixes\n- Let a slipping estimate go unspoken until the deadline\n:::\n\n::: check WHEN AN ESTIMATE SLIPS\n- Flag it the day you know, not the day it's due\n- Say why, in one plain sentence\n- Give a revised range, not a new single number\n- Update the tasks so the board tells the truth\n:::\n\nEstimation is a skill you sharpen by comparing your guesses to outcomes. After a piece of work lands, glance back: where was the time actually spent? The gap between your estimate and reality is the most useful feedback you'll get.\n\n::: stats\n- Scope first :: no number without a written \"done\"\n- Whole thing :: build + review + QA + deploy + fixes\n- Ranges :: honest uncertainty over false precision\n- Named risk :: the slip is a sentence, not a surprise\n- Tracked :: the board reflects reality daily\n\n:::\n",
  "security-baseline": "Foundry holds real client data — proposals, bank details, support conversations, contracts. Security isn't a feature we bolt on; it's the floor every change stands on. These are the non-negotiables. None of them are hard. All of them are the difference between a normal Tuesday and a very bad week.\n\n## Authentication and access\n\nThere are two gates, and you need to understand both. The outer gate is the `API_KEY` in middleware: every `/api/` route requires it except `/api/health`. Browser sessions on `/app/**` carry a `gitwork_api_session` cookie; external and iOS callers send `Authorization: Bearer {API_KEY}`. That gate proves the request is from us — it says nothing about *who* is calling.\n\nThe inner gate is per-user permission, resolved from a role matrix plus per-member overrides, and it's your responsibility in the handler:\n\n```ts\nassertCan(await getEffectiveUserOrNull(request), canManageClients, \"create clients\");\n```\n\nNew capabilities default **off**. They don't exist in the role matrix until switched on per person in Settings → Team. Super Admins bypass; everyone else is denied until granted. When you add a sensitive action, add a permission for it — don't lean on the fact that \"only admins have the button\".\n\n::: check EVERY SENSITIVE ROUTE\n- Authenticated via middleware (`API_KEY`) — no exceptions but `/api/health`\n- Authorised with `assertCan` before any read of sensitive data\n- Authorised with `assertCan` before every mutation\n- Scoped to the caller's `workspaceId`\n- New capability defaults off, granted explicitly\n:::\n\n## The cardinal rule\n\nEverything the client sends is a lie until you've checked it. The request body, query params, headers, a resource ID in the URL, the \"role\" field someone slipped into the JSON — all of it is attacker-controlled. Hiding a button, disabling a field, or filtering a list on the frontend is UX, not security. The server is the only boundary that matters.\n\n> The client is never trusted. Validate on the server or it isn't validated.\n\nThis is why permission checks live in the route, not the component. A hidden control is a courtesy to honest users; the `assertCan` is what stops the dishonest one who's reading your API in the network tab.\n\n## Validate every input\n\nUntrusted input meets Zod before it meets your logic. Parse the body against a schema from `src/server/validators.ts`, and `fromError` turns a `ZodError` into a clean 400 automatically.\n\n```ts\nconst body = clientCreateSchema.parse(await request.json());\n```\n\nValidation is also authorisation's quiet partner: re-check ownership on the resolved ID, not just the shape. A well-formed request for someone else's record is still an attack. Confirm the row belongs to the caller's workspace before you touch it — see [Data and Prisma conventions](/app/handbook).\n\n::: avoid NEVER TRUST\n- A resource ID without re-checking it belongs to the caller\n- A body field like `role` / `workspaceId` / `price` sent by the client\n- Frontend hiding as a substitute for a server check\n- Model or third-party output passed unvalidated into a query\n- User input interpolated into raw SQL or a shell command\n:::\n\n## Least privilege, everywhere\n\nGrant the minimum, drop the rest. A route returns only the fields the viewer may see — sensitive figures like rates and client financials are stripped in the DTO for viewers without the permission, never merely hidden in the UI. Sensitive data at rest (client bank details) is encrypted with AES-256-GCM via `src/lib/encryption.ts`. Tokens are stored per-connection server-side, never in `localStorage`. Logs never contain secrets, keys, or customer PII.\n\n::: check LEAST PRIVILEGE MEANS\n- DTOs omit fields the viewer can't access\n- Sensitive data encrypted at rest\n- Secrets in the VPS `.env`, never git or Slack\n- Third-party tokens server-side, scoped, minimal\n- No PII or secrets in logs\n:::\n\nFor where secrets live and the `ENCRYPTION_KEY` rule, read [Handling secrets and credentials](/app/handbook).\n\n## When you find a hole\n\nYou will, eventually — a route missing `assertCan`, a query without a workspace scope, a field leaking in a DTO. The right move is calm and fast, not quiet.\n\n::: steps\n- Don't exploit it further\n- Tell Dan / the channel immediately\n- Assess the blast radius: what could leak, how far back\n- Patch and ship the fix as the priority\n- Note it so we prevent the class, not just the case\n:::\n\nFinding a vulnerability is good work, not an embarrassment. We're blameless about how it got there and relentless about closing it. A hole reported today is a story; a hole exploited next month is an incident — see [Incident response](/app/handbook).\n\n::: stats\n- Gated :: auth then authorise, every route\n- Untrusting :: the client is never believed\n- Validated :: Zod on every input\n- Least-privilege :: minimum grant, minimum leak\n:::\n",
  "standups-and-communication": "We're a distributed team building for clients who can't watch us work. That means communication isn't overhead on top of the job — it *is* the job's other half. A change nobody can see the shape of, a blocker nobody hears about for two days, a client left guessing: these cost more than any bug. This is how we keep everyone — teammates and clients — confident that the work is moving.\n\n## Two standups a day\n\nFoundry runs standups in the [Desk](/app/handbook) and posts them to Slack. There are two, and they do different jobs. The AM standup sets intent — what you're picking up today — and on Mondays it pre-fills from tasks due that ISO week. The PM standup records reality — what you actually landed. Neither is a status meeting. They are two-minute writes, not a call.\n\n::: steps\n- AM: what I'm doing\n- Work the day\n- PM: what I got done\n- Blockers, loudly\n:::\n\nDevs and staff post standups; admins and super-admins don't (there's no \"standup pending\" nag for them). Your update lands in the involved clients' internal channels automatically, and the DevOps lead publishes one consolidated roll-up once everyone's in. So write your standup for a human on the client's account, not for a form.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> A blocker is not a PM standup footnote. The moment you're stuck for more than ~30 minutes on something you can't unstick alone, say so in the channel. Waiting for standup to surface a blocker wastes half a day.\n\n## Async-first, by default\n\nWe assume the reader is asleep, in another timezone, or heads-down. Good async writing means nobody has to schedule a call to understand you.\n\n::: check GOOD ASYNC WRITING\n- States the outcome first, context second\n- Links the task, PR or [document](/app/handbook)\n- Says what you need and by when\n- Is self-contained — no \"as discussed\"\n- Ends with a clear next step or owner\n:::\n\n::: avoid THINGS THAT BREAK ASYNC\n- \"Quick question?\" with no question\n- Burying the ask under three paragraphs\n- Threads that never resolve to a decision\n- @here for something one person owns\n- Silent disappearance mid-task\n:::\n\nReach for a call when the thread has bounced more than twice, when it's sensitive, or when you're aligning on shape before building. Then post the outcome back in writing — a decision that lives only in someone's memory didn't happen.\n\n> The client isn't buying code. They're buying the confidence that the thing they can't see is under control.\n\n## Talking to clients\n\nClients read tone as much as content. The standard is simple: tell the true thing early, in plain language, and never let them find a problem before you've named it. A slipped estimate said on Tuesday is a normal update; the same slip discovered by the client on Friday is a trust problem. See [scoping and estimation](/app/handbook) for how we keep those promises honest in the first place.\n\nKeep client comms in the client's channels and the [Portal](/app/handbook) task board, not in DMs — the account, not any one person, owns the relationship. When you hand a piece of work over, make sure the [handover checklist](/app/handbook) is done so the next person (or the client) isn't stranded.\n\n::: check EVERY CLIENT UPDATE\n- Leads with status: on track / at risk / blocked\n- Plain English, no internal jargon\n- Names any slip before they notice it\n- Says the next milestone and date\n- Written in British English\n:::\n\n## The standard\n\nCommunication at Gitwork is judged by one test: could a teammate pick this up cold, or a client feel informed, from what you wrote — without asking you a single follow-up?\n\n::: stats\n- Twice daily :: AM intent, PM reality\n- Async-first :: assume the reader's asleep\n- Blockers loud :: within 30 minutes, not at standup\n- True thing early :: name slips before clients do\n- In writing :: decisions that outlive the call\n:::\n",
  "welcome-how-we-work": "Welcome to Gitwork. We're a design-and-build software agency, and Foundry — the platform you're reading this in — is the machine we use to run it. Before you touch a line of code, read this. It's not a mission statement to skim past; it's how we actually operate, and it's what makes the difference between a good agency and a forgettable one.\n\nWe build software for clients who are trusting us with their business. That trust is fragile, it's earned slowly, and it's the only thing we really sell. Everything below is downstream of protecting it.\n\n## The five principles\n\nThese aren't posters. They're the decisions we make every day, written down so a new person can make the same ones without asking.\n\n::: grid\n### Ship small, ship often\nSmall changes are easy to review, easy to test and easy to roll back. A huge PR is a huge risk. Break the work down until each piece is boring to merge.\n### Own the outcome\nYour job isn't \"write the code\". It's \"the client's problem is solved and stays solved\". You own it through review, deploy, and the bug you find a week later.\n### Leave it better\nEvery file you open is a chance to improve it. Fix the typo, tidy the naming, delete the dead code. The codebase should get healthier because you were here.\n### Say the true thing early\nSlipping? Stuck? Wrong approach? Say so today, not on Friday. Bad news travels badly when it travels late. Early honesty is a gift to everyone downstream.\n:::\n\nThe fifth ties the rest together: **the client's trust is the product.** A clever feature that erodes it is a loss. A boring feature that reinforces it is a win.\n\n> We don't sell code. We sell the confidence that when we ship something, it works — and that when we say it's done, it's done.\n\n## What good looks like\n\nYou'll know you've internalised the principles when your default behaviour matches this list without thinking about it.\n\n::: check WHAT GOOD LOOKS LIKE\n- Changes small enough to review over coffee\n- You tested it yourself before asking anyone else to\n- Commit messages that read like a changelog\n- Problems raised the day you spot them, not the day they're due\n- The file you touched is tidier than you found it\n- Nothing reaches a customer that you wouldn't put your name on\n:::\n\n## How the work flows\n\nFoundry is one app with many modules, and the rhythm is the same across all of them. You pick up work from the [task board in Portal](/app/handbook), you build on a branch, and every push to `main` deploys straight to production in about six minutes. That speed is a privilege, and it only works because everyone respects the gates around it.\n\n::: steps\n- Pick up a task\n- Build on a branch\n- Self-test everything\n- Open a small PR\n- Squash-merge to main\n- Watch it deploy\n:::\n\nTwo habits keep that rhythm honest. First, our [Definition of Done](/app/handbook) is shared and non-negotiable — \"done\" means done, not \"works on my machine\". Second, we communicate async and early through the [AM/PM standups](/app/handbook), so nobody has to chase you to find out where things stand.\n\n::: avoid THIS ISN'T US\n- Heroic all-nighters to hide a slip\n- \"It's basically done\" (it either is or isn't)\n- Big-bang PRs nobody can review\n- Going quiet when you're stuck\n- Blaming the last person who touched the file\n:::\n\n## Where to go next\n\nRead these in order over your first day or two. They turn the principles above into concrete practice.\n\n- [First-week checklist](/app/handbook) — your path to a first merged, deployed PR\n- [Local environment setup](/app/handbook) — get Foundry running on your machine\n- [Code style and conventions](/app/handbook) — the house rules for how we write things\n- [Definition of Done](/app/handbook) — the shared meaning of \"finished\"\n\nWe hire sharp people and give them real ownership fast. The principles are what let us do that safely. Learn them, and the rest of the handbook is just detail.\n\n::: stats\n- Ship small, often :: risk shrinks with size\n- Own the outcome :: through deploy and beyond\n- Leave it better :: every file, every time\n- Say the true thing early :: bad news ages badly\n- Trust is the product :: everything protects it\n:::\n",
  "working-with-ai-providers": "AI is a dependency like any other network call — except it's slower, costs real money per token, and occasionally refuses to answer at all. Foundry treats it accordingly: every model is resolved per-workspace, every call assumes it can fail, and every prompt is written to be cheap. Here's how we call models across Pulse, Study, Scribe, Care, and Docs without hardcoding a thing.\n\n## Never hardcode a model\n\nThe active provider and model live on the workspace, configurable in Settings. A hardcoded `\"claude-sonnet-5\"` string is a bug: it ignores a workspace that's chosen OpenAI or a local LLM, and it dodges the single source of truth for defaults. Resolve, don't inline.\n\n```ts\nimport { resolveAiConfig, completeText, parseJsonObject } from \"@/server/ai-provider\";\n\nconst config = resolveAiConfig(workspace); // provider + apiKey + model + baseUrl\nconst raw = await completeText({\n  config,\n  system: \"You extract structured data. Return JSON only.\",\n  user: transcript,\n  tier: \"light\", // routes to Haiku / gpt-4o-mini for cheap classification\n});\nconst result = parseJsonObject<MySchema>(raw);\n```\n\n`resolveAiConfig` handles Anthropic (default), OpenAI, Gemini, and a local Ollama endpoint, with env vars taking precedence over workspace-stored keys. The fallback models live in one place — `DEFAULT_MODELS` in `ai-provider.ts` — and every resolver reads from it. If you're writing a `model:` literal, you're doing it wrong.\n\n## Assume it fails\n\nA model call is the least reliable line in your function. It can time out, run out of key, return empty text, or get declined by a safety classifier — modern Anthropic models return HTTP 200 with `stop_reason: \"refusal\"` and empty content rather than an error. `completeText` throws on a missing key and on refusal so you get a clear signal instead of silent emptiness. Your job is to catch it and degrade gracefully.\n\n::: check EVERY AI CALL HANDLES\n- No API key configured → clear \"set up AI in Settings\" error\n- Timeout / network failure → caught, feature still usable\n- Empty or malformed output → `parseJsonObject` returns null, you have a fallback\n- Refusal (`stop_reason: \"refusal\"`) → surfaced, not swallowed\n- Slow response → runs async so the user isn't blocked\n:::\n\nPulse is the model here: the deterministic checks run first and persist, then AI runs *on top*. An AI failure leaves a fully usable checks-only result rather than a dead scan. Design so the AI is the enhancement, not the load-bearing wall.\n\n> Treat the model like a flaky intern: brilliant when it works, and never the only thing standing between a customer and a broken page.\n\n## Never trust the output\n\nModel output is untrusted input — the same suspicion you'd apply to a request body. It's non-deterministic, it hallucinates fields, and it wraps JSON in prose or ```json fences. Parse defensively with `parseJsonObject` (it strips fences and extracts the object), then validate the result with Zod before it touches the database or the UI.\n\n::: avoid NEVER WITH AI OUTPUT\n- `JSON.parse` the raw response and trust it\n- Render model text as HTML without sanitising\n- Persist a model's structured output unvalidated\n- Pass model output into a query or shell command\n:::\n\n## Spend like it's your money\n\nTokens cost money, and a careless prompt run in a loop across every client adds up fast. Two levers keep the bill sane, both already wired into the helper.\n\n| Lever | How | When |\n|---|---|---|\n| Model tier | `tier: \"light\"` → Haiku / mini (~3.75× cheaper) | Classification, tagging, short summaries |\n| Prompt caching | Automatic on Anthropic — system prompt marked `cache_control` | Repeated calls sharing a system prompt |\n| `maxTokens` | Set it to what you actually need | Every call — don't leave headroom you won't use |\n\nUse the light tier for anything that doesn't need full reasoning quality. Keep a stable system prompt across a burst of calls so caching kicks in at $0.30/MTok reads instead of $3.00. And cap `maxTokens` — you pay for what the model *could* generate as much as what it does.\n\n## House rules\n\n- Resolve config once per request, reuse it across calls.\n- Long-running AI belongs in an async path (`after()`, a background job, or a cron), never blocking the response.\n- Log failures with enough context to debug, but never log the prompt if it contains customer data — see [Security baseline](/app/handbook).\n- API keys are secrets: env vars or the workspace record, never git. See [Handling secrets and credentials](/app/handbook).\n\n::: stats\n- Resolved :: model from workspace, never inlined\n- Resilient :: every call assumes failure\n- Validated :: output is untrusted input\n- Cheap :: light tier, caching, capped tokens\n:::\n",
  "writing-great-pull-requests": "A pull request is a piece of writing aimed at a reviewer. Its job is to make the change obvious, safe to approve, and fast to merge. The code is only half of it — the other half is helping another human understand *what* you changed, *why*, and *how you know it works*. Get that right and reviews take minutes, not days.\n\n## Keep it small\n\nThe single biggest lever on review quality is size. A 40-line PR gets a real read; a 900-line PR gets a rubber stamp. Small PRs are easier to reason about, easier to revert, and easier to deploy — which matters because on Foundry [every push to `main` deploys](/app/handbook).\n\nIf a change is genuinely large, split it: land the schema first, then the API, then the UI. Each piece should be independently correct and mergeable. If you can't split it, say so in the description and give the reviewer a reading order.\n\n> A PR should do one thing. If you're using the word \"and\" to describe it, it's probably two PRs.\n\n## Write the description\n\nThe diff shows *what* changed. The description explains *why* — the context no one can reconstruct from code alone. Fill in this template every time:\n\n```md\n## What\nOne or two sentences. What does this change do?\n\n## Why\nThe problem, ticket, or user need. Link it.\n\n## How\nKey decisions, trade-offs, anything non-obvious in the diff.\n\n## Testing\nHow you verified it. What you checked. What you didn't.\n\n## Screenshots / notes\nBefore/after for any UI. Follow-ups or known gaps.\n```\n\nTwo minutes of writing here saves the reviewer twenty minutes of archaeology. If a decision looks odd in the diff, pre-empt the question — leave a comment on your own PR explaining it before anyone asks.\n\n## Title it like a commit\n\nWe squash-merge, so **your PR title becomes the single commit on `main`** — which becomes the deploy-feed label everyone reads. Make it a proper [Conventional Commit](/app/handbook): a type prefix, a scope, and a clear imperative summary.\n\n```\nfeat(desk): surface @mentions in the Needs You Today rail\nfix(tasks): scope bulk-assign picker to the client's devs\nchore(deps): bump prisma to 6.2\n```\n\nNot `updates`, not `fixes`, not `WIP final v2`. If you can't write a clean one-line title, the PR probably isn't focused enough yet.\n\n## Before you request review\n\nRead your own diff first — the whole thing, top to bottom, as if you were the reviewer. You'll catch the stray `console.log`, the commented-out block, the debug tweak you meant to undo. Because there's no local database, [verify with `tsc` and `eslint`](/app/handbook) rather than assuming the build is green.\n\n::: check BEFORE REQUEST REVIEW\n- Diff read end to end\n- `npx tsc` and `eslint` clean\n- Debug logging & test data removed\n- Description template filled in\n- Title is a Conventional Commit\n- Screenshots for any UI change\n- Schema changes are additive only\n- Scoped to one thing\n:::\n\n::: avoid DON'T MAKE THE REVIEWER\n- Guess why you did it\n- Read 600 lines to find the point\n- Trip over your leftover `console.log`\n- Discover the WIP commits you forgot\n- Review unrelated drive-by changes\n:::\n\n## During review\n\nA review is a conversation, not a verdict. Reply to every comment — even if it's just \"done\" or \"good catch\". If you disagree, say why, kindly; the goal is the best code, not being right. Push follow-up commits to the branch (don't force-push mid-review — it wrecks the reviewer's \"what changed since I last looked\" view). Once approved, you merge your own PR: the author owns the change all the way to production.\n\nKeep the loop tight. A PR that sits open for three days rots — it drifts from `main`, collects conflicts, and blocks whoever's waiting on it. If review stalls, nudge in the channel. See [Code review standards](/app/handbook) for the reviewer's side of this, and [Branching & commits](/app/handbook) for how the branch itself should be shaped.\n\n::: stats\n- Small :: reviewable in one sitting\n- Clear :: the why is written down\n- Green :: tsc + eslint pass\n- Titled :: a clean Conventional Commit\n- Owned :: author sees it to production\n:::\n",
};
